export const supportingArticles: SupportingArticle[] = [
  {slug:"panic-attack-treatment-kathmandu",title:"Panic Attack Treatment Kathmandu",description:"Panic attack treatment in Kathmandu: symptoms, emergency signs, diagnosis, CBT and personalised psychiatric care for panic disorder in Nepal.",keywords:["Panic attack treatment Kathmandu","Panic disorder Nepal","Psychiatrist for panic attacks"],h1:"Panic Attack Treatment in Kathmandu",overview:"A panic attack is a sudden, intense surge of fear or discomfort that reaches its peak within about ten minutes and can feel exactly like a life-threatening medical emergency, which is part of what makes it so frightening. It typically strikes without an obvious external trigger, though it can also occur in a feared situation or, less commonly, wake someone from sleep. A single panic attack is common and does not automatically mean a person has a psychiatric disorder. Panic disorder develops when attacks recur and are followed by weeks of persistent worry about having another one, fear of the attacks’ consequences, or significant changes in behaviour to avoid them. With an accurate diagnosis and a structured treatment plan, panic disorder responds well to treatment and most people see a real reduction in both the frequency and intensity of attacks.",symptoms:"During an attack the body’s alarm system fires as though facing real danger, producing a racing or pounding heart, chest tightness or pain, shortness of breath, sweating, trembling, and a sense of choking or smothering. Many people also describe dizziness, lightheadedness, nausea, hot flushes or chills, tingling in the hands and lips, and a strange sense of unreality about themselves or their surroundings known as derealisation or depersonalisation. The psychological symptoms can be as distressing as the physical ones: a sudden conviction that something catastrophic is happening, such as a heart attack, stroke or loss of control, along with an overwhelming urge to escape. Attacks can occur unexpectedly, in specific situations, or occasionally during deep sleep, waking the person in a state of fear. Because the physical symptoms genuinely overlap with cardiac and respiratory emergencies, new chest pain, fainting, one-sided weakness or severe unexplained breathlessness should always be assessed medically rather than assumed to be panic, particularly for a first episode.",causes:"Panic reflects a false alarm in the body’s threat-detection system rather than any real danger, and it typically involves a self-reinforcing loop between physical sensations and catastrophic interpretation. A normal bodily sensation, such as a slightly faster heartbeat after climbing stairs or jitteriness after caffeine, is misread by the brain as a sign of imminent danger, which triggers a genuine stress response and produces more of the very sensations that felt alarming in the first place. Biological vulnerability, family history, heightened sensitivity to internal bodily sensations, chronic stress, poor sleep, excess caffeine, nicotine, alcohol withdrawal, and certain physical illnesses can all lower the threshold for an attack. Once a person starts avoiding places associated with past attacks, or relies on safety behaviours such as always carrying medication, the disorder is usually reinforced rather than resolved, because avoidance prevents the person from ever learning that the feared catastrophe does not occur.",assessment:"A psychiatric assessment for panic maps the pattern of attacks in detail: their frequency, typical triggers or lack thereof, physical symptoms, catastrophic thoughts during an episode, anticipatory anxiety between attacks, and any places or activities that have come to be avoided. It also reviews caffeine, nicotine, alcohol and other substance use, current medicines, and any physical symptoms that warrant medical investigation, since conditions such as thyroid disease, arrhythmias, asthma and vestibular disorders can produce genuinely similar sensations. Because panic frequently overlaps with other conditions, the assessment also screens for generalised anxiety, social anxiety, health anxiety, depression and trauma-related symptoms, all of which change the treatment plan. A basic physical examination or targeted investigations, arranged with a physician when indicated, are often a reasonable part of a first assessment, particularly when symptoms began later in life.",treatment:"Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) tailored to panic disorder is the best-established psychological treatment and usually runs over a structured course of weekly sessions. Early sessions focus on psychoeducation about the panic cycle and breathing retraining; the central technique is interoceptive exposure, in which the person deliberately and safely brings on the physical sensations they fear, such as brief spinning to trigger dizziness or breathing through a straw to trigger breathlessness, so the brain gradually relearns that these sensations are uncomfortable but not dangerous. Where avoidance has developed, situational exposure to avoided places is added in a graded, collaborative way. Cognitive work targets catastrophic misinterpretations, such as ‘this means I am dying’, and replaces them with more accurate appraisals built from the person’s own experience during exposure. Medication, typically an SSRI, may be considered after individual psychiatric assessment for more severe or persistent presentations, usually alongside therapy; benzodiazepines are used cautiously and briefly, if at all, given dependence risk.",local:"For a first severe panic attack, or whenever new or unexplained physical symptoms are involved, an in-person assessment in Kathmandu, Lalitpur or Bhaktapur allows a proper physical check alongside the psychiatric one, which matters most when the presentation is unfamiliar or alarming. Once serious medical causes have been reasonably excluded and a treatment plan is underway, regular CBT and medication follow-up can often continue through online consultation, which suits people balancing work, study or travel across Nepal. Patients from Pokhara, Biratnagar, Dharan and further afield frequently combine an initial in-person visit with ongoing video follow-up for convenience. Many workplaces and educational institutions in Kathmandu are increasingly willing to accommodate medical leave for mental health reasons, which can make it easier to attend an initial appointment without extended disruption to studies or employment.",related:["/anxiety","/online-psychiatrist-nepal","/conditions/panic-disorder"],faqs:[{question:"How is a panic attack different from a heart attack?",answer:"Both can cause chest pain, a racing heart and breathlessness, which is exactly why new or first-time chest pain should always be checked urgently by a doctor rather than assumed to be panic. Features that lean toward panic include a rapid peak within minutes and previous similar episodes with a normal medical work-up, but this distinction should be made by a clinician, not by the person experiencing the symptoms."},{question:"Can panic attacks happen during sleep?",answer:"Yes. Nocturnal panic attacks can wake a person from sleep in a state of sudden fear with a racing heart and breathlessness, without any dream or nightmare preceding it. They are treated the same way as daytime panic attacks, though they can feel more disorienting because there is no obvious trigger."},{question:"Does having panic attacks mean I have an anxiety disorder?",answer:"Not necessarily. A single panic attack, especially during unusual stress, illness or sleep deprivation, is common and does not by itself mean someone has panic disorder. The diagnosis is generally considered when attacks recur and are followed by persistent worry about future attacks or noticeable changes in behaviour to avoid them."},{question:"What should I do if I feel a panic attack starting?",answer:"Slowing the breath, especially lengthening the out-breath, naming what is happening (‘this is panic, it will pass, it is not dangerous’), and staying in the situation where it is safe to do so all help over time. These skills are useful for short-term relief but work best alongside structured therapy."},{question:"Can panic disorder be cured completely?",answer:"Many people who complete a course of CBT for panic disorder become free of attacks or have only occasional, much milder episodes. Others have a good response with some residual vulnerability during high-stress periods. Outcomes vary, which is why ongoing follow-up and relapse-prevention skills are part of a complete treatment plan."},{question:"Is medication necessary to treat panic attacks?",answer:"No. Many people respond well to CBT alone. Medication is one option considered on an individual basis, generally for more severe or frequent panic, and the decision is made collaboratively after a full psychiatric assessment rather than being automatic."}]},
  {slug:"social-anxiety-treatment-nepal",title:"Social Anxiety Treatment Nepal",description:"Social anxiety treatment in Nepal: understand social phobia, fear of public speaking, CBT, diagnosis and when to see a psychiatrist in Kathmandu.",keywords:["Social anxiety Nepal","Social phobia Kathmandu","CBT social anxiety"],h1:"Social Anxiety Disorder Treatment in Nepal",overview:"Social anxiety disorder, sometimes called social phobia, is a persistent and marked fear of being watched, judged or embarrassed in social or performance situations, well beyond ordinary shyness or occasional nervousness. It can centre on specific situations such as public speaking or eating in front of others, or it can be more generalised, touching most interactions from small talk to job interviews. The fear is usually recognised by the person as excessive, but it feels impossible to simply reason away, and the anticipation of a situation is often as distressing as the situation itself. Left unaddressed, it commonly narrows a person’s education, career and relationships over years rather than resolving on its own, which is why early, structured treatment matters.",symptoms:"Physically, social anxiety can produce blushing, sweating, trembling, a racing heart, nausea, a shaky voice, and a sudden blank mind when speaking is expected. Behaviourally, people often rehearse conversations in advance, avoid eye contact, arrive early or late to avoid mingling, decline invitations, or use alcohol to get through social events. A key feature is heightened self-focused attention: the person becomes intensely aware of how they appear, monitoring their own blushing or trembling in a way that makes the very symptoms they fear more likely. Many people become skilled at hiding the internal experience, so they can appear calm or even confident to others while feeling overwhelmed inside, which is why the true impact is best judged by its effect on education, work and relationships rather than by outward appearance.",causes:"Social anxiety usually develops from a combination of temperament, such as an innately cautious or behaviourally inhibited style noticeable from early childhood, and learning through experience, including being teased, criticised, excluded or harshly judged during formative social situations. Family patterns of social avoidance or overprotection can model and reinforce fearful responses to social risk. A cycle then sets in: avoidance and safety behaviours such as rehearsing every sentence bring short-term relief but prevent the person from ever testing whether the feared outcome, such as visible humiliation, would actually happen, so the fear is never disconfirmed and the disorder is maintained. Over time, the range of avoided situations can slowly expand, a pattern sometimes called safety-behaviour creep, where what began as avoiding one specific feared event gradually generalises to avoiding an increasing number of related situations.",assessment:"A psychiatric assessment maps the specific situations that are feared, from small daily interactions to high-stakes performance events, along with the safety behaviours and avoidance patterns used to get through them. It is important to distinguish social anxiety disorder from panic disorder occurring in social settings, generalised anxiety, depression, autism spectrum traits presenting with social difficulty, past trauma, and the effects of alcohol used to cope, since each changes the treatment approach. The assessment also considers how long symptoms have been present, since a diagnosis generally requires a persistent pattern rather than situational nervousness before a single event, and it explores the functional cost in education, employment and relationships. Because many people with social anxiety have learned to mask their distress convincingly, clinicians often ask directly about internal experience during specific recent situations rather than relying solely on how composed the person appears in the room.",treatment:"CBT is the leading evidence-based treatment and centres on graduated exposure: building a hierarchy of feared social situations from mildly to highly anxiety-provoking, and working through them in a planned order so avoidance is gradually replaced with real experience of coping. Behavioural experiments test specific feared predictions, such as ‘everyone will notice I am blushing and think less of me’, against what actually happens. Attention retraining helps shift focus away from self-monitoring and back onto the conversation or task at hand, and video feedback is sometimes used so a person can compare their actual appearance with their harsh internal self-image. Medication, most often an SSRI, can be considered for more severe or longstanding presentations after individual assessment, particularly when symptoms are too disabling to begin exposure work comfortably.",local:"This pattern is common among students facing presentations and viva examinations, young professionals navigating meetings and interviews, and Nepalis who have returned from abroad and are re-adjusting to different social expectations. In-person CBT in Kathmandu allows in-session practice such as role-play and real-time feedback, while online follow-up can support people in Pokhara, Biratnagar, Dharan and beyond who cannot easily travel for regular sessions. Many people also find it helpful to practise specific exposure tasks, such as ordering food in a restaurant or making a phone call, between sessions in their own environment, which video-based coaching can support just as effectively as in-person review.",related:["/anxiety","/contact","/conditions/social-anxiety-disorder"],faqs:[{question:"Is social anxiety just extreme shyness?",answer:"Shyness is a common personality trait that does not usually stop someone from functioning; social anxiety disorder is a clinical condition where fear of judgement is intense enough to cause significant distress or to limit school, work or relationships. Many shy people are never clinically anxious in this way."},{question:"Can social anxiety be treated without medication?",answer:"Yes. CBT with graded exposure is a first-line, standalone treatment for many people and does not require medication to be effective. Medication is one option considered individually, generally for more severe presentations or alongside, not instead of, therapy."},{question:"Why does avoiding social situations make the anxiety worse over time?",answer:"Avoidance brings quick relief, which makes it very reinforcing, but it also prevents the brain from ever learning that the feared outcome does not usually happen. Each avoided situation keeps the underlying fear intact, and situations often become harder to approach the longer they are avoided."},{question:"Will people notice if I am anxious in a social situation?",answer:"Research on this exact question consistently finds that visible signs of anxiety, such as blushing or a shaky voice, are typically far less obvious to others than they feel to the person experiencing them. This gap between internal experience and external appearance is itself a common target of therapy."},{question:"Can children and teenagers have social anxiety disorder?",answer:"Yes, it often begins in childhood or adolescence and can be mistaken for simple shyness rather than a treatable condition. Early recognition and support can prevent years of avoided opportunities in school and social life."},{question:"How long does treatment for social anxiety usually take?",answer:"There is no fixed duration, since it depends on severity, the number of feared situations and how consistently exposure exercises are practised between sessions. Many structured CBT courses run over a number of weeks to months, with progress reviewed along the way."}]},
  {slug:"generalized-anxiety-disorder-nepal",title:"Generalized Anxiety Disorder Nepal",description:"Generalized anxiety disorder in Nepal: excessive worrying, symptoms, diagnosis, CBT and personalised treatment with a psychiatrist in Kathmandu.",keywords:["Generalized anxiety disorder Nepal","GAD treatment Kathmandu","Excessive worrying"],h1:"Generalized Anxiety Disorder: Symptoms and Treatment",overview:"Generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) is characterised by excessive, difficult-to-control worry occurring on most days for six months or longer, rather than worry tied to one specific fear. The content often drifts across many areas of life at once — health, finances, family safety, work performance, or minor daily decisions — so that as one worry eases, attention moves to the next. People with GAD frequently describe an inability to simply switch off worry even when they recognise, intellectually, that it is not proportionate to the actual risk. Because the worry is chronic rather than acute, GAD is sometimes under-recognised, mistaken for a personality trait or a stressful phase, when it is in fact a treatable psychiatric condition. GAD is one of the more common anxiety presentations seen in general psychiatric practice, and it frequently coexists with other conditions such as depression, which is why a broad initial assessment is useful even when worry is the most prominent complaint.",symptoms:"Alongside the worry itself, GAD typically involves restlessness or feeling on edge, muscle tension, fatigue that is out of proportion to activity, irritability, difficulty concentrating or a sense that the mind goes blank, and sleep disturbance, particularly trouble falling asleep because the mind keeps working through worries. Many people also experience physical symptoms driven by chronic muscle tension and autonomic arousal, including headaches, jaw clenching, stomach discomfort, and a persistent low-grade sense that something bad is about to happen even when nothing specific has triggered it. Because these physical symptoms can dominate the picture, GAD sometimes presents first to a general physician rather than a mental health professional. Children and adolescents with GAD may present somewhat differently, often described by parents as constant reassurance-seeking or repeated physical complaints before exams or new situations, rather than describing worry directly in words.",causes:"GAD develops through an interaction of inherited vulnerability, temperament such as a tendency toward intolerance of uncertainty, and life experience, including chronic stress, unpredictable environments earlier in life, and periods of prolonged sleep disruption. A key maintaining factor is the belief, often unconscious, that worrying is protective or helps prevent bad outcomes, which leads people to keep worrying even though it rarely changes what happens and instead keeps the nervous system in a persistent state of alert. This is a genuine difficulty with a demonstrable psychological and physiological basis, not a matter of personal weakness or insufficient willpower. Life transitions common in Nepal, such as preparing for major examinations, migrating for work, or managing family financial expectations, can act as sustained stressors that bring an underlying vulnerability to worry into full clinical expression.",assessment:"Diagnosis is clinical and considers the duration of symptoms, typically six months or more, the breadth of worry topics, and the degree of functional impairment in work, relationships or daily tasks. The assessment distinguishes GAD from major depression, panic disorder, OCD, health anxiety, PTSD, and adjustment reactions to a specific ongoing stressor, since these have different treatment emphases despite overlapping features. Physical contributors such as thyroid disease, excess caffeine intake, certain medicines, and substance use are also considered, particularly when physical symptoms are prominent or onset is later in life without an obvious psychological trigger. The clinician also asks about the person’s own theories of what is driving the worry, since correcting specific unhelpful beliefs about the function of worrying is often central to later treatment.",treatment:"CBT for GAD typically works on two connected fronts: helping the person notice and step back from the worry process itself, including experiments to test beliefs like ‘worrying keeps my family safe’, and building tolerance for uncertainty rather than seeking the impossible goal of eliminating all risk. Problem-solving skills are taught for worries that relate to genuinely solvable issues, while acceptance and attention-shifting techniques are used for hypothetical or unanswerable worries. Sleep protection, caffeine reduction and regular physical activity are useful supporting steps. Medication, most commonly an SSRI or SNRI, can be considered after individual psychiatric assessment for moderate to severe or longstanding GAD, usually reviewed over weeks rather than judged after only a few days.",local:"A structured course of CBT can be started with an in-person consultation in Kathmandu or through an online appointment for patients elsewhere in Nepal, since worry-focused therapy translates well to video sessions. Ongoing review is useful because GAD tends to wax and wane with life stress, so periodic check-ins help catch relapse early rather than after months of accumulated distress. Many patients find that simple, consistent check-ins, even brief ones, help maintain the gains made during the initial course of therapy.",related:["/anxiety","/mental-health-screening","/conditions/generalized-anxiety-disorder"],faqs:[{question:"How is GAD different from just being a worrier?",answer:"Many people describe themselves as worriers without having a disorder. GAD is diagnosed when worry is excessive relative to the actual situation, hard to control, present on most days for six months or more, and accompanied by physical symptoms or functional impairment, not simply a personality trait."},{question:"Can GAD occur alongside depression?",answer:"Yes, the two frequently occur together, and each can worsen the other. Assessment specifically screens for depressive symptoms because the treatment plan, and sometimes the choice of medication, may need to address both conditions together."},{question:"Does GAD ever go away on its own?",answer:"It can fluctuate with life circumstances, sometimes easing during calmer periods, but for many people it persists as a longstanding pattern without treatment. Structured therapy and, where appropriate, medication meaningfully change its course rather than requiring the person to simply wait it out."},{question:"Is worrying about real problems, like finances or health, still part of GAD?",answer:"Yes. GAD does not require the worries to be unrealistic; the issue is the excessive intensity, duration and uncontrollability of the worry relative to the actual level of risk, along with the physical and functional toll it takes."},{question:"Can lifestyle changes alone treat GAD?",answer:"Sleep, exercise, and reduced caffeine and alcohol intake can meaningfully ease symptoms and support other treatment, but for a clinical diagnosis of GAD, structured therapy such as CBT and, for some people, medication are usually needed for a full and lasting response."},{question:"Will I need to take medication indefinitely?",answer:"Not necessarily. Many people take medication for a defined period alongside therapy and later taper off under medical supervision, while others benefit from longer-term treatment. This is an individual decision reviewed periodically rather than a lifelong default."}]},
  {slug:"how-to-choose-best-psychiatrist-nepal",title:"How to Choose a Psychiatrist in Nepal",description:"How to choose a psychiatrist in Nepal: qualifications, communication, confidentiality, online care and questions to ask before a mental health consultation.",keywords:["Best psychiatrist Nepal","Psychiatrist Kathmandu","How to choose psychiatrist"],h1:"How to Choose the Best Psychiatrist in Nepal",overview:"There is no ethical, evidence-based way for any clinician or website to declare one doctor the single ‘best’ psychiatrist for everyone, because the right fit depends on the specific concern, the patient’s preferences, and practical factors such as location and language. A more useful question is whether a psychiatrist is properly qualified, listens carefully, explains diagnosis and options in plain language, and proposes a plan tailored to the individual rather than a one-size-fits-all prescription. Choosing a psychiatrist is also a relationship decision, not only a technical one, since ongoing treatment often depends on feeling heard and respected over repeated visits. Word-of-mouth recommendations can be a useful starting point, but they work best combined with the practical questions below rather than substituted for them entirely.",symptoms:"This article is not about symptoms of an illness but about recognising when it is time to seek a consultation at all. Persistent low mood, severe or constant worry, panic, sleep disruption, problematic substance use, thoughts of self-harm, unusual beliefs or perceptions, marked mood swings, or a noticeable decline in work, study or relationships are all reasonable, common reasons to book an assessment. A good clinician will spend the first meeting clarifying what is actually happening rather than immediately proposing a fixed treatment or medication before understanding the full picture. It is also reasonable to seek an assessment simply because a friend, teacher or family member has expressed concern, even if the person themselves is unsure whether anything is genuinely wrong.",causes:"People often delay seeking psychiatric care because of stigma, uncertainty about cost, difficulty travelling to a clinic, family disapproval, or simply not knowing what a consultation involves or whether their concern is ‘serious enough’ to warrant one. Conflicting advice from well-meaning friends, family or online sources can add to the hesitation. Clear, honest information about what a psychiatric consultation actually looks like, and reassurance that concerns do not need to reach crisis level before being taken seriously, meaningfully lowers this barrier to seeking help. In many Nepali families, concerns about how a diagnosis might affect marriage prospects or social standing add a further, culturally specific layer of hesitation that a respectful clinician should acknowledge rather than dismiss.",assessment:"Before booking, it is reasonable to ask about a psychiatrist’s specialist qualifications and registration, their experience with the specific concern, how confidentiality is protected, what happens in a genuine emergency, the likely cost and structure of follow-up, and whether a trusted family member can be involved with the patient’s consent. A transparent clinician will answer these questions directly rather than being evasive, and will explain, in the first session, what assessment they plan to do and why, so the patient understands the reasoning behind any recommendation. It is also reasonable to ask how the psychiatrist typically communicates between appointments, for instance whether urgent questions can be raised by phone or message, since this varies between practices and matters for ongoing care.",treatment:"Quality psychiatric care typically combines several elements as needed: psychotherapy such as CBT, practical lifestyle and sleep guidance, family psychoeducation, and medication only when it is genuinely indicated by the diagnosis and severity, not as a default first step. Patients should be cautious of any provider who promises a guaranteed cure, prescribes medication without a proper assessment, or dismisses questions about diagnosis, side effects or alternatives. A good treatment plan is explained clearly enough that the patient understands what is being tried, why, and how progress will be reviewed. A clinician who welcomes a second opinion, rather than treating it as a challenge to their judgement, is generally a good sign of confidence in their own reasoning and respect for the patient’s autonomy.",local:"For people in Kathmandu Valley or cities such as Pokhara and Chitwan, proximity to a clinic may be an important practical factor, particularly if in-person follow-up or physical examination is likely to be needed. For others, especially Nepalis living in more remote areas or abroad, an online psychiatric consultation can be a practical and clinically appropriate first step for many non-emergency concerns, with local in-person care available when needed. Whichever format is chosen, continuity with the same clinician over time tends to produce better outcomes than switching frequently between providers.",related:["/best-psychiatrist-nepal","/online-psychiatrist-nepal","/contact"],faqs:[{question:"Do I need a referral to see a psychiatrist in Nepal?",answer:"Generally no; most psychiatric clinics in Nepal, including online consultation services, accept direct self-referral without needing a letter from another doctor, though a referral can be useful if you are already under investigation for a related physical condition."},{question:"What is the difference between a psychiatrist and a psychologist or counsellor?",answer:"A psychiatrist is a medical doctor with specialist training who can diagnose mental illness, order relevant investigations, and prescribe medication when appropriate, alongside offering or coordinating therapy. Psychologists and counsellors are trained in assessment and talking therapies but do not prescribe medication; many patients benefit from working with both."},{question:"Will everything I say in a psychiatric consultation stay confidential?",answer:"Yes, with narrow, clearly explained exceptions where there is a serious risk to the patient’s or someone else’s safety, which any ethical clinician should discuss with you openly rather than as a surprise. Confidentiality is a basic expectation of psychiatric care."},{question:"How do I know if a psychiatrist is properly qualified?",answer:"Ask directly about their specialist psychiatric qualification (such as an MD in Psychiatry), their registration with the relevant medical council, and their experience with your specific concern. A legitimate clinician will answer these questions clearly and without discomfort."},{question:"Is it normal to see more than one psychiatrist before finding the right fit?",answer:"Yes, this is reasonable, particularly if communication feels poor or the treatment plan is not adequately explained. Continuity of care has value, but it should not come at the cost of feeling unheard or rushed."},{question:"Can I bring a family member to my first appointment?",answer:"Many patients find this helpful, and most psychiatrists welcome it with the patient’s consent, since family input can add useful history and support the treatment plan. The choice of whether and how much to involve family remains the patient’s to make."}]},
  {slug:"depression-vs-normal-sadness",title:"Depression vs Normal Sadness",description:"Depression versus normal sadness: signs of clinical depression, diagnosis, treatment and urgent warning signs explained by a psychiatrist in Nepal.",keywords:["Depression vs sadness","Signs of depression","Clinical depression"],h1:"Depression vs Normal Sadness",overview:"Sadness is a normal, expected human response to loss, disappointment, failure or hardship, and it typically eases over days to weeks as circumstances change or the person adapts. Clinical depression, or major depressive disorder, is different: it is a persistent syndrome lasting two weeks or more that affects mood, energy, thinking and the body, and it does not reliably lift just because good things happen or circumstances improve. Depression can occur with or without an obvious trigger, and its severity is judged not by how ‘sad’ someone looks but by the breadth of symptoms and the degree to which daily functioning, relationships and self-care are affected. Some people continue to function outwardly at work or in social settings, sometimes described as ‘smiling depression’, while privately experiencing significant internal distress, which is one reason depression should never be ruled out simply because someone appears to be coping.",symptoms:"Beyond low mood, depression commonly involves a marked loss of interest or pleasure in activities that were previously enjoyable, persistent fatigue, feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt, poor concentration and indecisiveness, and changes in sleep and appetite in either direction. Hopelessness about the future and, in more severe cases, thoughts of death or self-harm can occur and always warrant direct, prompt attention. Depression does not always look like visible sadness; it can present as irritability, unexplained physical aches, social withdrawal, or simply going through the motions of daily life while feeling numb or empty inside, which is why it is sometimes missed in people who appear to be coping.",causes:"Depression arises from an interplay of biological vulnerability, including genetic and neurochemical factors, significant life events such as loss, conflict or trauma, chronic physical illness, social isolation, and disrupted sleep, rather than from any single cause. It is not evidence of personal failure, weakness, ingratitude, or a lack of willpower, and telling someone to simply ‘think positive’ does not address the underlying biological and psychological processes involved. Understanding depression as a genuine medical condition, comparable to other illnesses that affect the body’s systems, is central to reducing the shame that often delays people from seeking help. Cultural narratives that frame depression as a spiritual failing or a punishment can further compound this shame in Nepal, even though the condition responds to the same evidence-based treatments used worldwide.",assessment:"A psychiatric assessment establishes how long symptoms have lasted, how severely they affect daily functioning, and whether there is any risk of self-harm, which is always asked about directly and compassionately rather than avoided. It reviews previous depressive episodes, current medicines and physical health, and substance use, since these can mimic or worsen depression. Critically, the assessment also screens for any past periods of unusually elevated mood, energy or risk-taking, because these would suggest bipolar disorder, which needs a different treatment approach than depression alone, particularly regarding antidepressant use. It also considers the person’s own understanding of what has changed, since patients and family members sometimes describe different aspects of the same episode.",treatment:"Evidence-based treatments include structured psychotherapy such as CBT, behavioural activation (systematically rebuilding activity and engagement even before motivation returns), and interpersonal therapy, along with practical support for sleep, routine and social connection. Medication, typically an antidepressant, may be appropriate for moderate to severe depression after individual assessment, usually taking several weeks to show its full effect and requiring a genuine trial period rather than early judgement. Treatment should be reviewed over time, with adjustments made if progress stalls, rather than stopped as soon as there is a brief improvement, since early discontinuation is a common cause of relapse. Combining therapy and medication tends to produce better outcomes than either alone for moderate to severe depression, though the right balance depends on the individual’s severity, preferences and past response to treatment.",local:"People in Nepal are encouraged to seek an assessment before symptoms become severe rather than waiting for a crisis, since earlier treatment is generally easier and more effective. Online follow-up can be a practical option when regular travel from cities such as Butwal, Biratnagar or Dharan to Kathmandu is difficult, with in-person care available when a first assessment or safety concern requires it. Family members are often the first to notice withdrawal or a drop in functioning, and their observations, shared with the person’s consent, can meaningfully speed up an accurate assessment.",related:["/depression","/contact","/conditions/major-depressive-disorder"],faqs:[{question:"How long does sadness need to last before it might be depression?",answer:"A general guide is symptoms lasting most of the day, nearly every day, for two weeks or more, alongside other features such as loss of interest, sleep or appetite change, or poor concentration, rather than sadness alone. A proper assessment weighs the full pattern rather than duration in isolation."},{question:"Can someone be depressed without feeling sad?",answer:"Yes. Depression can present as numbness, irritability, physical fatigue, unexplained aches, or simply a flat loss of interest in life, without the person describing themselves as sad. This is one reason depression can go unrecognised, including by the person experiencing it."},{question:"Does depression always need medication?",answer:"No. Many people with mild to moderate depression respond well to psychotherapy alone. Medication is one option considered individually for moderate to severe presentations, and the decision depends on severity, past response and personal preference, not on a fixed rule."},{question:"Can depression come back after successful treatment?",answer:"Yes, depression can recur, which is why relapse-prevention strategies, and sometimes longer-term maintenance treatment, are part of a complete plan. A previous episode does not mean a future one is inevitable, but it is a reason to know the early warning signs."},{question:"Is it possible to have depression even if my life looks fine from the outside?",answer:"Yes. Depression is not caused only by external circumstances, and outward success, a supportive family or a comfortable life do not protect against it. It reflects changes in brain function and chemistry that can occur regardless of how a person’s life appears from outside."},{question:"When does sadness or grief need professional help?",answer:"When it is unusually prolonged, severely impairs daily functioning, involves thoughts of self-harm, or feels disproportionate to what triggered it, professional assessment is reasonable. Grief and depression can also occur together and sometimes benefit from separate, tailored attention."}]},
  {slug:"signs-you-should-see-a-psychiatrist",title:"When to See a Psychiatrist",description:"When to see a psychiatrist in Nepal: signs that a mental health consultation, psychiatric assessment or urgent in-person care may be needed.",keywords:["When to see a psychiatrist","Mental health consultation","Psychiatric assessment"],h1:"Signs You Should See a Psychiatrist",overview:"Seeing a psychiatrist is appropriate whenever symptoms are persistent, distressing, recurrent, or interfering with daily life, and a person does not need to be in crisis, or have a confirmed diagnosis in mind, before requesting an assessment. Many people wait months or years before seeking help because they assume their difficulty is not ‘serious enough’, when in fact early assessment often makes treatment simpler and more effective. A consultation is a chance to have a trained clinician help make sense of confusing or overwhelming symptoms, not a step reserved only for the most severe presentations. Some people also seek an assessment during a period of significant life transition, such as starting university, a new job, or becoming a parent, even without a clear symptom in mind, simply to build support proactively.",symptoms:"Common reasons to book an assessment include persistent low mood or loss of interest, severe or constant worry, panic attacks, significant sleep disruption, escalating alcohol or substance use, thoughts of self-harm, unusual beliefs or perceptions such as hearing things others do not, marked and uncharacteristic mood swings, memory or concentration difficulties that affect daily tasks, or a noticeable decline in functioning at work, study or home. None of these symptoms need to be present in isolation or at their most extreme to justify a consultation; a gradual decline noticed by the person or by family is equally valid grounds to seek assessment.",causes:"Psychiatric symptoms can arise from a primary mental health condition, an underlying physical illness such as thyroid disease or anaemia, medication side effects, alcohol or drug use, acute or chronic stress, or some combination of these, and it is often impossible to tell which without a proper evaluation. This is precisely why self-diagnosis from internet searches or social media, while a reasonable starting point for information, is not a substitute for a clinical assessment that can weigh the full picture, including physical health, together. A basic set of blood tests is sometimes a reasonable part of a first assessment specifically to rule out simple, treatable physical contributors such as anaemia or thyroid dysfunction before attributing symptoms purely to a psychological cause.",assessment:"A psychiatric assessment is a structured but conversational process exploring the current symptoms, their timeline, past psychiatric and medical history, relevant family history, current stresses and supports, safety, and the person’s own goals for the consultation. It is collaborative rather than a test to be passed or failed, and it carries no judgement about a person’s character, intelligence or worth. Confidentiality applies throughout, with narrow, clearly explained exceptions where safety is at serious risk, and the person remains an active participant in decisions about their own care. First appointments are often longer than routine medical visits specifically to allow enough time to build an accurate, complete picture, and this should not be mistaken for the consultation being unusually complicated or serious.",treatment:"The outcome of a first consultation varies considerably depending on what is found: it might be psychoeducation and reassurance, a course of psychotherapy, practical lifestyle and sleep guidance, referral for physical health investigations, a medication review, or a combination of these. Not every consultation results in a prescription, and a good clinician will explain their reasoning rather than defaulting to medication as a first response. Some concerns are resolved within a few sessions, while others benefit from a longer, structured course of treatment reviewed over time. Whatever the plan, the person should leave the first appointment with a clear understanding of what happens next and roughly when to expect a follow-up review.",local:"In-person psychiatric care is available in Kathmandu for a thorough first assessment, particularly when physical symptoms, safety concerns, or an unclear picture make an in-person evaluation more appropriate. Online consultation can be a convenient and clinically suitable option for selected non-emergency concerns and follow-up across Nepal, though anyone in crisis should go to the nearest emergency department rather than wait for a scheduled appointment. Anyone unsure whether their situation counts as an emergency can call the clinic to ask, rather than trying to make that judgement alone.",related:["/contact","/online-psychiatrist-nepal","/mental-health-screening"],faqs:[{question:"Do I need to be ‘severely ill’ to see a psychiatrist?",answer:"No. Many people who consult a psychiatrist have mild to moderate symptoms that are affecting their quality of life but are far from a crisis. Earlier assessment often makes treatment more straightforward, so waiting for symptoms to become severe is not necessary."},{question:"What if I am not sure whether my problem is ‘psychiatric’?",answer:"That uncertainty is a completely normal reason to book a consultation. Part of a psychiatrist’s role is to help figure out what is actually happening, including whether a physical cause needs investigation, rather than requiring the patient to arrive with a self-diagnosis."},{question:"Will a psychiatrist automatically put me on medication?",answer:"No. Many consultations result in psychotherapy, lifestyle guidance or simple monitoring rather than medication. When medication is discussed, it should be explained clearly, including why it is being considered and what the alternatives are."},{question:"Can I see a psychiatrist just to talk to someone, without a specific diagnosis in mind?",answer:"Yes. Many people book an initial consultation simply because something feels wrong or they want an objective assessment, without needing to name a specific condition beforehand. Clarifying the problem is part of what the assessment is for."},{question:"What should I bring to a first psychiatric consultation?",answer:"It helps to bring a list of current medicines, any relevant medical reports, a rough timeline of when symptoms started, and, if comfortable, a family member who knows the history well. None of this is mandatory, and the appointment can still proceed usefully without it."},{question:"How urgently should I seek help if I am having thoughts of self-harm?",answer:"Thoughts of self-harm or suicide should always be taken seriously and shared with a trusted person or clinician promptly. If there is any immediate danger or a plan to act on these thoughts, this is an emergency requiring immediate in-person care rather than waiting for a scheduled appointment."}]},
  {slug:"sleep-problems-and-mental-health",title:"Sleep Problems and Mental Health",description:"Sleep problems and mental health: understand insomnia, anxiety, depression, assessment and evidence-based help from a psychiatrist in Nepal.",keywords:["Sleep problems mental health","Insomnia psychiatrist Nepal","Poor sleep anxiety"],h1:"Sleep Problems and Mental Health",overview:"Sleep and mental health influence each other in both directions: poor sleep can intensify anxiety, low mood, irritability and difficulty concentrating, while anxiety, depression and other psychiatric conditions commonly disturb sleep in return, creating a cycle that can be hard to break without addressing both sides. Sleep difficulty is one of the most common symptoms across almost all psychiatric conditions, which means persistent sleep problems deserve their own careful assessment rather than automatically being dismissed as ‘just stress’ or treated only with sleeping tablets. Because sleep affects mood, memory, concentration and even physical health over the long term, addressing it early is a reasonable priority in its own right, not only as a side effect of treating something else.",symptoms:"Insomnia can involve difficulty falling asleep, frequent night-time waking, waking too early and being unable to return to sleep, or sleep that feels unrefreshing despite adequate time in bed. Other sleep problems need different questions altogether: loud snoring with witnessed breathing pauses can suggest obstructive sleep apnoea, an uncomfortable urge to move the legs at night suggests restless legs syndrome, and recurrent distressing nightmares may point toward trauma-related symptoms. Shift work and irregular schedules can disrupt the body’s internal clock in ways that mimic insomnia but require a different, timing-focused approach.",causes:"Common contributors include stress, anxiety, depression, chronic pain, caffeine and alcohol use, irregular sleep and wake times, and excessive evening screen use, all of which can independently or jointly disturb sleep architecture. Some sleep problems have primary medical causes, including sleep-disordered breathing, thyroid dysfunction, and certain medications, and should not be treated purely as a psychological issue or as a sign of overthinking. Long-standing poor sleep habits, such as spending excessive time in bed awake, can also become self-perpetuating independent of whatever originally triggered the disturbance.",assessment:"Assessment reviews the specific pattern and timing of sleep difficulty, its impact on daytime functioning, current medicines, caffeine, alcohol and other substance use, and mood and anxiety symptoms that may be driving or resulting from poor sleep. Screening questions for snoring, witnessed breathing pauses, leg discomfort at night and daytime sleepiness help identify sleep-disordered breathing or restless legs syndrome, which may need referral for a sleep study or specialist medical review rather than psychiatric treatment alone. A short sleep diary kept over one to two weeks before the consultation, recording bedtime, wake time and night-time awakenings, often adds more useful information than memory alone can provide.",treatment:"Cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I), which addresses sleep-related thoughts, habits and scheduling, is the first-line, best-evidenced treatment for chronic insomnia and works for many people without medication. Alongside this, treating any underlying anxiety, depression or pain condition is usually essential for durable improvement. A consistent sleep and wake schedule, limiting time spent awake in bed, and reducing evening caffeine and alcohol are useful supporting steps. Medication for sleep can be considered after individual assessment for specific situations, but sedative-hypnotics are not intended as a universal or indefinite solution given tolerance and dependence risks with prolonged use.",local:"Online follow-up works well for CBT-I, which relies heavily on sleep diaries, structured homework and regular review rather than physical examination, making it convenient for patients across Nepal. Urgent symptoms, or features suggesting sleep apnoea such as loud snoring with witnessed breathing pauses and excessive daytime sleepiness, are better assessed in person, sometimes with referral for a sleep study. Patients who travel frequently for work can still maintain a CBT-I programme online, since the core techniques rely on consistent daily habits rather than a fixed location.",related:["/anxiety","/depression","/conditions/insomnia"],faqs:[{question:"Can poor sleep actually cause anxiety or depression, or only result from it?",answer:"Both directions are well established. Chronic sleep deprivation can independently worsen mood regulation and anxiety, while anxiety and depression commonly disrupt sleep. In practice, most cases involve some degree of both, which is why treatment usually needs to address sleep and mood together."},{question:"Is it safe to use sleeping tablets long-term?",answer:"Most sleeping medications are intended for short-term or occasional use because of risks including tolerance, dependence and reduced effectiveness over time. Long-term insomnia is generally better managed with CBT-I and treatment of underlying causes, with medication used selectively."},{question:"How much sleep do I actually need?",answer:"Needs vary between individuals, but most adults function best with somewhere in the range of seven to nine hours. The more useful marker than a fixed number is how a person feels and functions during the day, since some people do well on slightly less and others need more."},{question:"Can I have insomnia and sleep apnoea at the same time?",answer:"Yes, they can coexist, and one can mask or worsen the other. This is one reason a proper assessment asks specifically about snoring, breathing pauses and daytime sleepiness rather than treating all sleep complaints as straightforward insomnia."},{question:"Does napping during the day make insomnia worse?",answer:"Long or late-afternoon naps can reduce the pressure to sleep at night and worsen insomnia for some people, while short, early naps may not have the same effect. This varies enough between individuals that it is usually addressed specifically within CBT-I."},{question:"When should sleep problems be checked in person rather than online?",answer:"Loud snoring with witnessed breathing pauses, excessive uncontrollable daytime sleepiness, sudden onset of severe insomnia with other new symptoms, or any suspicion of a sleep-disordered breathing problem are best assessed in person, sometimes with referral for further sleep testing."}]},
  {slug:"stress-management-professionals-nepal",title:"Stress Management for Professionals Nepal",description:"Stress management for professionals in Nepal: recognise burnout, protect sleep, manage work stress and know when mental health support is needed.",keywords:["Stress management Nepal","Work stress","Burnout"],h1:"Stress Management for Professionals in Nepal",overview:"For many working professionals, high workload, financial responsibility, long commutes, caregiving duties and limited recovery time combine to make stress feel like a permanent baseline rather than an occasional state. Effective stress management is not about becoming endlessly more productive or resilient; it is about protecting sleep, health and functioning over the long run, and recognising when ordinary work pressure has tipped into burnout or a clinical condition that needs more than a better routine. In Nepal’s growing service and technology sectors, an always-on communication culture, where messages are expected to be answered outside working hours, has made it particularly difficult for many professionals to establish a genuine boundary between work and rest.",symptoms:"Warning signs include persistent irritability, poor or unrefreshing sleep, tension headaches, physical exhaustion that does not improve with rest, reduced concentration, a growing sense of cynicism or detachment from work, and increasing use of alcohol, nicotine or other substances to unwind at the end of the day. Burnout specifically involves emotional exhaustion, a cynical or depersonalised view of one’s work, and a reduced sense of personal accomplishment, and it commonly overlaps with, or progresses into, clinical anxiety or depression rather than remaining a purely occupational issue.",causes:"Unclear boundaries between work and personal time, chronically excessive workload, workplace conflict or poor management, perfectionism, and inadequate genuine rest all contribute to burnout, and these factors compound each other over months or years rather than appearing suddenly. While individual coping strategies genuinely matter, organisational conditions, such as unsustainable expectations or lack of control over one’s own workload, are equally significant and cannot be fully offset by personal resilience alone. In smaller organisations, where a single person often carries multiple roles, the absence of backup support can make ordinary workload spikes feel disproportionately overwhelming.",assessment:"Assessment distinguishes ordinary work stress from clinical depression, generalised anxiety, substance use problems, sleep disorders and physical illnesses that can present with fatigue, since each requires a different response. Severity, duration, and the degree of functional impairment, together with any safety concerns, guide whether the appropriate plan is largely practical and lifestyle-focused or requires structured psychiatric treatment. Sleep quality is given particular weight in this assessment, since disrupted sleep is often both an early symptom of worsening stress and a factor that independently makes recovery harder.",treatment:"Useful steps include realistic renegotiation of workload where possible, protecting a consistent sleep schedule, regular physical activity, structured problem-solving for solvable stressors, and CBT-based skills for the thinking patterns that intensify stress, such as perfectionism or catastrophising about work outcomes. Professional support becomes important when stress symptoms persist despite these changes, or when they begin to resemble depression or anxiety in their own right. Immediate emergency help is needed for thoughts of self-harm or an inability to keep oneself safe, regardless of how manageable the underlying stress might otherwise seem.",local:"This is highly relevant to professionals in Kathmandu, Lalitpur, Biratnagar, Pokhara and other growing urban centres in Nepal, where long working hours and lengthy commutes often extend work stress directly into family and rest time. Online consultation can fit around demanding work schedules for both assessment and ongoing follow-up. Employers in Kathmandu are increasingly open to flexible scheduling around medical appointments, which can make it easier to attend a first consultation without needing to disclose the specific reason to colleagues.",related:["/contact","/online-psychiatrist-nepal"],faqs:[{question:"How is burnout different from ordinary tiredness?",answer:"Ordinary tiredness typically improves with rest and days off. Burnout involves a persistent pattern of exhaustion, cynicism about work, and reduced sense of accomplishment that does not resolve with a weekend or short holiday and tends to worsen without a genuine change in workload or coping."},{question:"Can burnout turn into depression?",answer:"Yes, prolonged burnout can develop into or coexist with clinical depression, particularly when exhaustion, hopelessness and loss of interest extend beyond work into personal life. This overlap is one reason persistent burnout symptoms deserve proper assessment rather than being managed with willpower alone."},{question:"Is stress management just about relaxation techniques?",answer:"Relaxation and mindfulness can help, but sustainable stress management usually also requires addressing workload, boundaries, sleep and unhelpful thinking patterns such as perfectionism. Relaxation techniques alone rarely resolve stress that is being driven by ongoing structural or workplace factors."},{question:"When should work stress be treated as a medical concern?",answer:"When symptoms persist despite reasonable changes to routine and workload, when they meet criteria for anxiety or depression, or when there are thoughts of self-harm, work stress should be assessed by a professional rather than managed alone indefinitely."},{question:"Can changing jobs solve burnout?",answer:"Sometimes, particularly if the current role is genuinely unsustainable, but burnout can also recur in a new role if the underlying patterns, such as perfectionism or difficulty setting boundaries, are not also addressed. Treatment often works best when it targets both the external situation and these internal patterns."}]},
  {slug:"mental-health-medical-students-nepal",title:"Mental Health for Medical Students",description:"Mental health for medical students in Nepal: burnout, exam anxiety, depression, sleep and when to seek confidential professional support.",keywords:["Mental health medical students","Burnout medical students","Exam anxiety"],h1:"Mental Health in Medical Students",overview:"Medical training in Nepal is intellectually demanding and can be deeply meaningful, but the combination of long clinical hours, high academic expectations, financial pressure, competitive assessments and repeated exposure to illness and suffering can genuinely affect a student’s mental health. Struggling under these conditions says nothing about a student’s ability, intelligence or commitment to the profession, and seeking support during training is increasingly recognised as a sign of good judgement rather than weakness. Institutions increasingly recognise that supporting student wellbeing is linked to safer, more competent future doctors, which is gradually helping to shift attitudes away from the assumption that struggling silently is simply part of the training.",symptoms:"Warning signs include persistent exhaustion that does not improve with rest, dread about attending clinical duties or classes, poor concentration, significant sleep loss, social withdrawal from friends and family, panic symptoms before exams or ward rounds, low mood, and recurring thoughts of leaving training altogether. Any thoughts of self-harm, however fleeting, are never ‘just part of the stress of medical school’ and always warrant urgent, confidential support rather than being pushed aside until after the next exam.",causes:"Burnout among medical students is shaped by heavy workload, prolonged uncertainty about outcomes, perfectionism reinforced by a highly competitive training culture, financial strain, and very limited time for genuine rest or recovery between rotations and examinations. Depression and anxiety frequently coexist with this stress and should not be automatically dismissed as ordinary exam nerves, since they represent a different and treatable clinical picture requiring more than time management alone. The transition from pre-clinical to clinical years, in particular, often brings a sharp increase in responsibility and exposure to distressing situations with comparatively little formal preparation for the emotional impact involved.",assessment:"A confidential psychiatric assessment considers the specific symptoms, their impact on studies and clinical performance, safety, sleep, substance use, and physical health, and works to distinguish a difficult but time-limited stressful period from a condition, such as depression or an anxiety disorder, that needs structured treatment. Confidentiality is particularly important for students who may worry, often unnecessarily, about how seeking help could affect their standing in training. The assessment also asks about coping strategies already being used, including any reliance on substances to manage stress, since this is common and best addressed openly rather than left unspoken.",treatment:"Helpful approaches include practical, realistic scheduling around clinical and academic demands, peer and mentor support, protecting sleep even during intense rotations, CBT-based skills for managing exam anxiety and perfectionistic thinking, and psychiatric care when symptoms meet the threshold for a treatable condition. Seeking help early, rather than waiting until a crisis point during a critical rotation or examination period, generally protects both the student’s wellbeing and their academic progress. Peer support groups specifically for medical students can also help normalise these struggles and reduce the isolation that often accompanies them.",local:"Students training in Kathmandu, Bharatpur, Dharan and other medical education centres in Nepal should use local emergency services immediately for any acute risk to safety rather than waiting for a scheduled or online consultation, while non-urgent concerns can often be managed with a combination of in-person and online follow-up around a demanding timetable. Clinical rotations based outside Kathmandu, such as in regional teaching hospitals, may need a hybrid plan combining occasional in-person review with online follow-up between postings.",related:["/contact","/depression"],faqs:[{question:"Will seeking mental health support affect my academic record or career prospects?",answer:"Confidential psychiatric consultation is separate from academic assessment and is not something that should be reported to your institution without your consent, except in narrow safety-related situations explained to you directly. Seeking help early is far more likely to protect your training than to harm it."},{question:"Is it normal to feel like leaving medical school during a difficult rotation?",answer:"Passing thoughts of this kind during an especially hard period are common and do not necessarily indicate depression, but persistent hopelessness, loss of interest, or an inability to function should be assessed rather than dismissed as ordinary stress."},{question:"How can I tell if I have exam anxiety or a genuine anxiety disorder?",answer:"Anxiety that is limited to the exam period and eases afterward is common; an anxiety disorder tends to be more persistent, broader in scope, and significantly impairing even outside exam periods. An assessment can help clarify which pattern fits your experience."},{question:"Can burnout during medical training be prevented?",answer:"It cannot always be fully prevented given the demands of the training, but protecting sleep, maintaining some non-academic activities and relationships, and seeking support early when symptoms first appear can reduce its severity and duration significantly."},{question:"Who can I talk to confidentially if I am struggling during training?",answer:"A psychiatrist outside your own training institution can offer a confidential assessment without direct involvement of your academic supervisors, which some students find easier than seeking help internally. Trusted peers, mentors and family can also be part of a support network alongside professional care."}]},
  {slug:"alcohol-addiction-treatment-nepal",title:"Alcohol Addiction Treatment Nepal",description:"Alcohol addiction treatment in Nepal: alcohol dependence, withdrawal risks, psychiatric assessment, family support and evidence-based recovery care.",keywords:["Alcohol addiction treatment Nepal","Alcohol dependence","Alcohol withdrawal"],h1:"Alcohol Addiction Treatment in Nepal",overview:"Alcohol dependence is a recognised, treatable medical condition rather than a moral failing or simple lack of self-control, though stigma in Nepal often makes it harder for people to seek help early. It typically involves a cluster of features including strong craving, difficulty controlling the amount or frequency of drinking, increased tolerance, withdrawal symptoms when not drinking, and continuing to drink despite clear harm to health, work, finances or relationships. Because alcohol is often woven into social and festive occasions in Nepal, the line between heavy social drinking and a genuine dependence can be harder for both patients and families to recognise without a structured clinical assessment.",symptoms:"Warning signs include needing a drink in the morning to feel normal or to stop shaking, repeated unsuccessful attempts to cut down, drinking in secret or lying about the amount consumed, memory blackouts during drinking episodes, visible tremor or sweating when alcohol wears off, disrupted sleep, and neglect of work or family responsibilities. Confusion, seizures, hallucinations, severe agitation or dangerously high heart rate after stopping or reducing alcohol are medical emergencies requiring immediate attention, because severe alcohol withdrawal can be life-threatening without proper management.",causes:"Genetic vulnerability, chronic stress, trauma, coexisting depression or anxiety, social and family drinking patterns, and easy availability all contribute to the development of alcohol dependence. Many people initially drink to cope with distress or to socialise more comfortably, only to find over time that alcohol itself worsens sleep, mood and anxiety, deepening reliance on it in a cycle that becomes progressively harder to break without support. Peer and family drinking norms, where refusing alcohol at social gatherings can feel awkward or be met with pressure to conform, can also make it genuinely difficult to cut down even when a person wants to.",assessment:"A thorough assessment covers the pattern, amount and duration of drinking, any prior withdrawal symptoms or seizures, current medicines and physical health, and coexisting depression, anxiety or suicide risk, alongside the strength of family and social support available. Whether medically supervised detoxification is needed is judged individually based on the severity of dependence and withdrawal history, since abrupt unsupervised withdrawal can be dangerous for people with significant physical dependence. Blood tests assessing liver function and nutritional status are often a useful part of this assessment, since chronic heavy drinking can affect the liver and cause nutritional deficiencies that need their own attention.",treatment:"Treatment can include medically supervised withdrawal management when indicated, medicines that reduce craving or support abstinence for some patients after individual assessment, motivational interviewing to strengthen the person’s own reasons for change, CBT for relapse prevention, family-inclusive intervention, and connection to peer support groups. Anyone with a likely significant dependence should not attempt abrupt withdrawal alone, given the risk of serious complications, and should seek medical guidance before stopping. Vitamin supplementation, particularly thiamine, is often part of safe withdrawal management, since deficiency during heavy drinking can otherwise contribute to serious neurological complications.",local:"Urgent withdrawal management should always take place in person, ideally in a hospital or clinical setting equipped to monitor and manage complications safely. Ongoing treatment, including relapse-prevention therapy and family support, can often be coordinated from Kathmandu with appropriate follow-up arranged for patients living elsewhere in Nepal once the acute phase has been safely managed. Families are often central to supporting the practical and emotional side of recovery in Nepal, and involving them, with the patient’s consent, is usually encouraged from an early stage.",related:["/contact","/online-psychiatrist-nepal","/conditions/alcohol-use-disorder"],faqs:[{question:"Is it dangerous to stop drinking suddenly if I drink heavily every day?",answer:"It can be, particularly for people with significant physical dependence, since severe alcohol withdrawal can cause seizures, severe confusion or other dangerous complications. Anyone drinking heavily and regularly should seek medical guidance before stopping rather than attempting abrupt withdrawal alone."},{question:"Does alcohol dependence mean someone lacks willpower?",answer:"No. Alcohol dependence involves real changes in brain chemistry and physical tolerance that make stopping far harder than willpower alone can overcome, which is exactly why it is treated as a medical condition rather than simply a matter of self-discipline."},{question:"Can alcohol dependence be treated without going to rehab?",answer:"Many people are treated successfully through outpatient care combining medical guidance, therapy and, for some, craving-reduction medication, particularly when dependence is moderate and support at home is available. More severe dependence or unsafe home environments may need inpatient or supervised care."},{question:"How is alcohol withdrawal different from a hangover?",answer:"A hangover is a short-lived, self-limiting response to a single episode of drinking. Alcohol withdrawal occurs in people with physical dependence after reducing or stopping regular heavy drinking and can include tremor, anxiety, sweating and, in severe cases, seizures or confusion requiring urgent medical care."},{question:"Can family be involved in treatment for alcohol dependence?",answer:"Yes, with the patient’s consent, family involvement often strengthens treatment through practical support, better understanding of the condition, and help maintaining a safer home environment during recovery. Family members can also benefit from guidance on how to support recovery without enabling continued drinking."}]},
  {slug:"internet-gaming-addiction-nepal",title:"Internet and Gaming Addiction Nepal",description:"Internet and gaming addiction in Nepal: warning signs, assessment, healthy digital habits and evidence-based mental health support for families.",keywords:["Internet addiction Nepal","Gaming addiction","Screen addiction"],h1:"Internet and Gaming Addiction",overview:"Problematic internet or gaming use is defined by loss of control and genuine harm, not simply by the number of hours spent online, and gaming disorder is formally recognised in the World Health Organization’s ICD-11 diagnostic system. A useful starting question for families is not ‘how much time’ but what the gaming or internet use appears to be doing for the person, since it often serves a real emotional function, such as escape, achievement or connection, that also needs to be understood.",symptoms:"Warning signs include repeated failed attempts to cut down or stop, significant sleep loss due to gaming or scrolling, missed study, work or family obligations, withdrawal from previously enjoyed offline activities and relationships, irritability or distress when access is interrupted or removed, and continuing the pattern despite clear negative consequences that the person themselves may acknowledge. The pattern over time is usually more telling than any single session length. Physical signs such as disrupted sleep patterns, eye strain, neglected meals, and reduced physical activity often accompany the behavioural pattern and are worth noting as part of the overall picture.",causes:"Games and social platforms are deliberately designed around variable rewards, achievement systems and social validation, which reinforces repeated checking and play in a way that can resemble other reward-based behaviours. For many users, particularly adolescents and young adults, gaming or scrolling also offers a genuine escape from anxiety, low mood, loneliness or family conflict, and the pattern becomes self-reinforcing as offline life feels comparatively less rewarding or more difficult to face.",assessment:"A psychiatric assessment looks at duration and functional harm rather than screen-time numbers alone, and screens for underlying depression, anxiety, ADHD, autism spectrum traits and substance use, since problematic gaming or internet use often accompanies or masks one of these conditions rather than existing in isolation. Not every enthusiastic or high-frequency gamer meets criteria for a disorder, and this distinction matters for how the family responds. Where the person is a minor, assessment also considers the home environment, parental supervision patterns, and whether the gaming or online activity is being used to manage difficulties at school or with peers.",treatment:"An effective plan usually involves collaborative, realistic limits agreed with the person rather than imposed unilaterally, structured offline activities and sleep and device boundaries, CBT-based work on the underlying urges and emotional needs the behaviour is meeting, and treatment of any coexisting depression, anxiety or ADHD. Sudden, punitive bans on devices often increase family conflict and secrecy without teaching the self-regulation skills that produce lasting change. Encouraging alternative sources of the same reward, such as sport, structured hobbies or social activities that offer genuine achievement and connection, tends to be more effective than restriction alone.",local:"Families in Nepal can often begin with one shared, realistic change, such as device-free mealtimes or a fixed overnight charging location outside the bedroom, before escalating to a fuller behavioural plan. A psychiatric assessment is worth arranging when functioning at school, work or home is clearly and persistently affected. Schools in Kathmandu and elsewhere are increasingly willing to coordinate with families and clinicians when academic performance has been affected, which can support a more consistent plan across home and school.",related:["/adhd","/contact","/conditions/gaming-disorder"],faqs:[{question:"How much gaming time is considered a problem?",answer:"There is no fixed hour threshold; the distinguishing feature is loss of control and genuine harm to sleep, relationships, study or work, not the raw number of hours. Two people with similar hours can have very different levels of impairment."},{question:"Is gaming disorder a real medical diagnosis?",answer:"Yes, gaming disorder is included in the World Health Organization’s ICD-11 classification, defined by impaired control over gaming, increasing priority given to gaming over other activities, and continuation despite negative consequences, persisting for at least twelve months in typical presentations."},{question:"Should parents just remove the device completely?",answer:"Abrupt, punitive removal often increases conflict and secrecy without addressing why the behaviour developed in the first place. Collaborative limit-setting, alongside understanding what need the gaming is meeting, tends to produce more lasting change than a sudden total ban."},{question:"Can problematic gaming be a sign of depression or anxiety in a teenager?",answer:"Yes, gaming can function as an escape from depression, anxiety, loneliness or family conflict, so an assessment for problematic use should also screen for these underlying conditions rather than treating the gaming itself as the only issue."},{question:"Does every heavy gamer need psychiatric treatment?",answer:"No. Many people game heavily without significant harm to their functioning, relationships or responsibilities. Treatment is considered when there is genuine loss of control and clear negative impact, not simply high engagement with the activity."}]},
  {slug:"postpartum-depression-nepal",title:"Postpartum Depression Nepal",description:"Postpartum depression in Nepal: baby blues, warning signs, diagnosis, treatment, family support and urgent maternal mental health care.",keywords:["Postpartum depression Nepal","Baby blues","Depression after childbirth"],h1:"Postpartum Depression in Nepal",overview:"The weeks and months following childbirth bring major hormonal, physical, emotional and social change, and some tearfulness, mood swings and overwhelm in the first two weeks, often called the ‘baby blues’, is common and usually resolves on its own. Postpartum depression is different: it is a persistent low mood, anxiety or loss of functioning that continues beyond this initial period, or emerges later in the first year, and it deserves the same serious clinical attention as depression occurring at any other time.",symptoms:"Symptoms include persistent sadness, excessive guilt about being ‘a bad mother’, severe anxiety or intrusive worry about the baby’s safety, poor sleep even during the baby’s sleep windows, loss of pleasure in activities including time with the baby, hopelessness, and difficulty forming an emotional bond with the infant. Frightening intrusive thoughts about harm coming to the baby are relatively common and are usually a symptom of the anxiety and distress rather than an intention, but any thoughts of self-harm, harm to the baby, severe confusion or unusual beliefs require emergency assessment without delay.",causes:"Rapid hormonal shifts after delivery, cumulative sleep deprivation, a difficult or traumatic birth experience, a personal or family history of depression or bipolar disorder, limited practical or emotional support, financial strain, and relationship difficulties can all contribute to postpartum depression, often in combination rather than through any single cause. In many Nepali households, expectations around a new mother’s role, pressure related to the baby’s sex, and reduced privacy or autonomy during the postpartum period can add further, culturally specific strain that is worth naming openly during assessment rather than overlooking.",assessment:"Assessment covers safety for both mother and infant, current infant care and bonding, any past mood episodes including possible mania or hypomania, screening for postpartum psychosis, physical health including thyroid function, and the practical support available at home. Asking directly about frightening thoughts regarding the baby is a necessary and compassionate part of assessment, intended to identify and help, never to judge or blame the mother. Input from a supportive family member, with the mother’s consent, can help build a fuller picture of daily functioning and safety, particularly when the mother herself finds it hard to describe how she is coping.",treatment:"Effective treatment can include psychological therapy such as CBT or interpersonal therapy, practical support to protect sleep and reduce caregiving burden, and individualised decisions about medication made in careful discussion with the mother, including consideration of breastfeeding where relevant, since several antidepressants are considered compatible with breastfeeding under specialist guidance. Family psychoeducation helps partners and relatives understand the condition and provide appropriate, non-judgemental support during recovery. Practical measures such as sharing night-time infant care where possible, arranging help with household tasks, and protecting even short blocks of uninterrupted sleep can meaningfully support recovery alongside formal treatment.",local:"A trusted family member or partner can help arrange urgent in-person assessment at the nearest hospital or clinic in Kathmandu or elsewhere in Nepal whenever there is any concern about safety, confusion, or unusual beliefs. For less urgent symptoms, a combination of in-person and online follow-up can support ongoing treatment around the demands of caring for a newborn.",related:["/depression","/contact","/conditions/postpartum-depression"],faqs:[{question:"How is postpartum depression different from the ‘baby blues’?",answer:"Baby blues typically appear in the first few days after birth, involve mild mood swings and tearfulness, and resolve within about two weeks without treatment. Postpartum depression is more persistent, more severe, and does not resolve on its own, requiring proper assessment and treatment."},{question:"Are frightening thoughts about harming the baby dangerous?",answer:"Intrusive, unwanted thoughts about the baby’s safety are relatively common in postpartum anxiety and depression and are usually distressing precisely because the mother does not want them to happen. They should always be disclosed to a clinician, who can distinguish this common symptom from the much rarer, more serious presentation of postpartum psychosis."},{question:"Can I take antidepressants while breastfeeding?",answer:"Several antidepressants are considered compatible with breastfeeding under specialist guidance, and this decision should always be made individually with a psychiatrist weighing the specific medication, the baby’s health, and the mother’s needs."},{question:"Can fathers or partners get postpartum depression too?",answer:"Yes, partners can experience a similar depressive syndrome after the birth of a child, sometimes linked to sleep loss, role changes and relationship strain, though it is less commonly recognised or screened for than depression in mothers."},{question:"How soon after birth can postpartum depression start?",answer:"It can begin anytime within the first year after childbirth, not only in the first weeks, and it can also develop gradually rather than suddenly, which is one reason ongoing awareness throughout the first year matters."},{question:"What is postpartum psychosis and how is it different from postpartum depression?",answer:"Postpartum psychosis is a rare but serious emergency involving confusion, unusual beliefs, hallucinations or extreme mood disturbance, and it requires immediate in-person emergency care. It is distinct from and much less common than postpartum depression, but any severe confusion or unusual beliefs after childbirth should be treated as urgent."}]},
  {slug:"adult-adhd-nepal",title:"Adult ADHD Nepal",description:"Adult ADHD in Nepal: attention difficulties, assessment, differential diagnosis, practical support and individualised psychiatric treatment in Kathmandu.",keywords:["Adult ADHD Nepal","Attention deficit adults","ADHD diagnosis Nepal"],h1:"Adult ADHD in Nepal",overview:"Adult ADHD can affect organisation, time awareness, task initiation, working memory, impulse control and emotional regulation, and it often persists from childhood even when it was never recognised or diagnosed at the time. Many adults are only identified after years of inconsistent performance despite genuine effort and intelligence, sometimes prompted by a child’s own ADHD diagnosis leading a parent to recognise similar lifelong patterns in themselves. In academic and work cultures that place a high value on discipline and consistent output, adults with undiagnosed ADHD often internalise years of criticism as a personal character flaw before an accurate diagnosis reframes the difficulty correctly.",symptoms:"Common experiences include chronically missed deadlines, forgetfulness in daily tasks, difficulty starting or finishing tasks that lack immediate interest, losing important items, interrupting others or struggling to wait their turn, inner restlessness, and difficulty sustaining attention on effortful, unstimulating work while sometimes hyperfocusing intensely on preferred activities. For a diagnosis, these patterns need to be longstanding, present since childhood in some form, and genuinely impairing across more than one area of life, rather than reflecting a temporarily busy or stressful period.",causes:"ADHD is a neurodevelopmental condition with strong inherited influences, reflecting differences in brain regions and networks involved in attention, impulse control and executive function. It is not caused by laziness, low intelligence, poor parenting or insufficient discipline, and framing it this way tends to increase shame without improving functioning, whereas understanding it as a difference in brain wiring opens the door to practical, effective strategies. Twin and family studies consistently show ADHD running in families, and it is common for a parent to recognise, once their own child is diagnosed, that they experienced very similar difficulties growing up without ever being assessed.",assessment:"Diagnosis requires a detailed developmental history looking for childhood-onset symptoms, current impairment across settings such as work and relationships, and careful consideration of other explanations for similar symptoms, including sleep deprivation, anxiety, depression, trauma, substance use and bipolar disorder, all of which can resemble ADHD but need different treatment. Standardised rating scales and, where possible, collateral information from family can support but do not replace clinical judgement. School reports or old academic records, when available, can sometimes provide helpful supporting evidence of childhood-onset difficulties even many years later.",treatment:"Treatment may include skills-based CBT for organisation and time management, practical coaching and environmental systems such as reminders and structured routines, treatment of any coexisting anxiety, depression or sleep problems, and medication considered only after individual psychiatric assessment, with ongoing monitoring for effectiveness and side effects. Stimulant and non-stimulant options both exist, and the right choice depends on the individual’s health history and response, reviewed over time rather than fixed at the first prescription.",local:"An initial assessment benefits from an in-person or thorough video consultation that can gather detailed developmental history, sometimes with input from a family member who remembers childhood patterns. Once a diagnosis and treatment plan are established, online follow-up can suit adults living outside Kathmandu for ongoing monitoring and adjustment. Workplace accommodations, such as flexible task structuring or written follow-up after meetings, can also be discussed as part of a practical, non-medication component of the overall plan.",related:["/adhd","/online-psychiatrist-nepal","/resources/adhd-stimulant-medications-guide"],faqs:[{question:"Can ADHD be diagnosed for the first time in adulthood?",answer:"Yes, adult ADHD diagnosis is common and does not require a childhood diagnosis on record, though the assessment does look for evidence that symptoms were present, even if unrecognised, since childhood. Many adults are diagnosed only after years of unexplained difficulty."},{question:"Does having ADHD mean someone is not intelligent?",answer:"No. ADHD affects attention regulation, organisation and impulse control, not intellectual ability, and many people with ADHD are highly capable in areas that interest them while struggling significantly with tasks that require sustained, unstimulating effort."},{question:"Is medication the only treatment for adult ADHD?",answer:"No. Skills-based therapy, coaching, and environmental and organisational strategies are valuable on their own or alongside medication. The right combination depends on symptom severity, personal preference and any coexisting conditions, decided individually."},{question:"Can anxiety or depression be mistaken for ADHD?",answer:"Yes, poor concentration and disorganisation can occur in anxiety, depression and after prolonged sleep deprivation, which is why a thorough assessment carefully distinguishes these from ADHD rather than assuming attention difficulties always indicate ADHD specifically."},{question:"How long does an adult ADHD assessment take?",answer:"It typically takes longer than a standard consultation because it requires a detailed developmental and functional history, sometimes across more than one session, particularly when collateral information from family is being gathered to confirm childhood-onset symptoms."}]},
  {slug:"memory-problems-dementia-assessment-nepal",title:"Memory Problems and Dementia Nepal",description:"Memory problems and dementia assessment in Nepal: causes of forgetfulness, red flags, diagnosis, family guidance and when to seek urgent care.",keywords:["Memory problems Nepal","Dementia assessment","Forgetfulness"],h1:"Memory Problems and Dementia Assessment",overview:"Forgetfulness has many possible causes, including stress, depression, poor sleep, certain medicines, and the ordinary, generally mild slowing of memory that can accompany ageing, and most people experience some of these without having dementia. Dementia describes a progressive decline in memory and other cognitive abilities significant enough to interfere with independent daily functioning, and it requires careful, structured assessment to distinguish from these more common and often treatable causes of forgetfulness. Because the early signs of dementia can be subtle and easily attributed to normal ageing, families are often the first to notice a genuine change in someone’s usual pattern of memory and functioning, and their observations are an important part of assessment.",symptoms:"Important clues include repeatedly asking the same questions, getting lost in familiar places, increasing difficulty managing finances or medicines correctly, word-finding and language difficulties, and noticeable personality or behavioural change. These symptoms typically develop gradually over months. Sudden confusion developing over hours to days is not typical of dementia and should be treated as a medical emergency, since it more often indicates delirium, an acute and usually reversible medical condition requiring urgent evaluation. Changes in mood or personality, such as increased apathy, suspiciousness or social withdrawal, can sometimes appear before memory problems become obvious and should not be dismissed as simply a normal part of ageing.",causes:"Alzheimer disease and vascular disease are among the most common causes of dementia, alongside other neurological conditions, but a substantial number of memory presentations have other explanations entirely, including depression (sometimes called pseudo-dementia because it can closely mimic cognitive decline), delirium from infection or medical illness, thyroid dysfunction, vitamin B12 deficiency, and side effects of certain medicines, several of which are treatable or reversible when identified early. Chronic conditions such as poorly controlled diabetes or long-standing high blood pressure can also contribute to vascular changes affecting memory over time, which is one reason general physical health is reviewed alongside cognitive symptoms.",assessment:"A proper assessment combines history from both the patient and a family member who knows them well, an evaluation of daily functioning such as managing money, medication and appointments, structured cognitive testing, a general medical review, and targeted investigations such as blood tests and, where indicated, brain imaging to look for treatable contributing causes. This process aims to respect the patient’s autonomy and dignity while also recognising the genuine burden often carried by family caregivers.",treatment:"Care typically includes identifying and treating any reversible contributing causes, cognitive and functional support strategies, structured family education about the expected course and how to communicate effectively, safety planning around driving, finances and home hazards, and individualised decisions about medication where appropriate for the underlying cause. Early diagnosis, even when the underlying condition cannot be reversed, supports better planning, safety and dignity for both the patient and the family over time. Structured daily routines, clear labelling around the home, and consistent caregivers can meaningfully support day-to-day functioning even when the underlying condition cannot be reversed.",local:"Families in Kathmandu or elsewhere in Nepal should seek urgent in-person medical care for any sudden confusion, fever, seizure, head injury, or abrupt change in level of consciousness, since these require emergency evaluation rather than a routine memory assessment. Planned assessment for gradual memory change can often begin with an in-person consultation, with family involved throughout given the importance of collateral history.",related:["/contact","/online-psychiatrist-nepal","/conditions/delirium"],faqs:[{question:"Is forgetfulness a normal part of ageing?",answer:"Some mild slowing in recalling names or misplacing items occasionally is common with normal ageing. Dementia is different: it involves progressive decline significant enough to interfere with independent daily functioning, such as managing money or medicines safely, which is not part of typical ageing."},{question:"Can depression look like dementia?",answer:"Yes, this is sometimes called pseudo-dementia, where depression in an older adult causes genuine difficulties with concentration and memory that closely resemble early dementia. Treating the underlying depression can significantly improve cognitive symptoms in these cases, which is why assessment considers both possibilities."},{question:"What is the difference between dementia and delirium?",answer:"Delirium develops suddenly, over hours to days, often due to an underlying medical illness such as infection, and is usually reversible with prompt treatment. Dementia develops gradually over months to years. Sudden confusion is always a medical emergency."},{question:"Are there treatments that can reverse dementia?",answer:"Most causes of dementia, such as Alzheimer disease, cannot currently be reversed, though some treatments can help manage symptoms and slow progression for certain conditions. Some causes of memory decline, such as thyroid problems or vitamin deficiency, are treatable or fully reversible, which is why thorough assessment matters."},{question:"Should someone with memory problems still be allowed to drive or manage finances?",answer:"This depends entirely on the individual’s specific abilities and the assessment findings, and is addressed directly and sensitively as part of safety planning. Gradual, dignified transitions of responsibility are usually preferable to either premature restriction or waiting until a crisis occurs."}]},
  {slug:"online-psychiatric-consultation-nepal",title:"Online Psychiatric Consultation Nepal",description:"Online psychiatric consultation in Nepal: telepsychiatry, privacy, suitable concerns, limitations and when urgent in-person care is necessary.",keywords:["Online psychiatrist Nepal","Telepsychiatry Nepal","Online mental health consultation"],h1:"Online Psychiatric Consultation in Nepal",overview:"Online psychiatric care can make assessment, follow-up, psychoeducation and medication review considerably more accessible for people who face barriers of distance, mobility, time or privacy at home, but it is appropriate only when the specific clinical situation genuinely suits a remote format and when adequate privacy can be maintained during the consultation itself. It is best understood as one valid mode of delivering care, not a lesser or lower-quality substitute for in-person psychiatry.",symptoms:"Online consultation is generally well suited to conditions such as anxiety, depression, sleep problems, ADHD follow-up, and other stable, non-emergency concerns where physical examination is not immediately required. Acute confusion, severe intoxication, suspected overdose, psychosis involving danger to self or others, or any situation involving immediate safety risk all require in-person emergency care and should never be managed solely through an online appointment. A useful general rule is that any situation where someone cannot be kept safe until the next available appointment needs an in-person emergency response rather than a scheduled video consultation.",causes:"Distance from a psychiatric clinic, demanding work schedules, mobility limitations, caregiving responsibilities, and living outside Kathmandu or major cities are common, legitimate barriers that prevent or delay people from accessing psychiatric care in Nepal. Telepsychiatry meaningfully reduces many of these practical barriers, but it does not remove the underlying need for a careful, thorough clinical assessment; convenience should never come at the cost of assessment quality. For patients in remote districts of Nepal, the nearest specialist psychiatric service may be many hours away, which makes reliable, well-conducted telepsychiatry a genuinely valuable option rather than a lesser alternative.",assessment:"At the start of an online consultation, the clinician confirms the patient’s identity and current location, checks that the environment offers adequate privacy for a confidential conversation, establishes emergency contact details in case they are needed, and assesses whether the presenting concern is genuinely suitable for video-based care. Patients are generally asked to have current medicines, relevant reports and their questions ready in advance to make the most of the appointment time.",treatment:"Online care can effectively deliver structured psychotherapy, psychoeducation, and ongoing monitoring of treatment response and side effects for many conditions. Prescribing decisions and any necessary physical health checks still require sound clinical judgement, and may need coordination with a local pharmacy, laboratory or physician, particularly for medicines requiring monitoring or when a physical examination becomes necessary as treatment progresses. Session structure, homework between appointments, and progress tracking can all be maintained just as consistently online as in person for suitable conditions.",local:"This model is particularly useful for people living in Pokhara, Butwal, Chitwan, Biratnagar, Dharan and other parts of Nepal outside Kathmandu, as well as for Nepalis living abroad seeking continuity of care, subject to what is clinically appropriate and legally permitted in each situation. Time zone differences for patients abroad are usually manageable with advance scheduling, and many clinics offer some flexibility around appointment times to accommodate this.",related:["/online-psychiatrist-nepal","/contact"],faqs:[{question:"Can a psychiatrist diagnose me accurately over a video call?",answer:"For many conditions, a thorough clinical interview conducted over video can support an accurate assessment, particularly for follow-up or conditions like anxiety and depression that rely heavily on verbal history rather than physical examination. Some presentations genuinely need in-person evaluation, and a responsible clinician will say so."},{question:"Is online psychiatric consultation confidential?",answer:"Yes, with the same confidentiality standards as in-person care and the same narrow, clearly explained exceptions for serious safety concerns. Patients are also advised to ensure a private space during the call itself, since this is part of protecting confidentiality on their end."},{question:"Can medication be prescribed through an online consultation?",answer:"In many cases yes, for conditions and situations where this is clinically appropriate, though some medicines and situations require in-person examination, monitoring or local coordination with a pharmacy, particularly early in treatment or for medicines needing regular physical monitoring."},{question:"What internet or technical setup do I need for an online consultation?",answer:"A stable internet connection and a private space are the main requirements; most consultations can be conducted through common video-calling platforms without specialised equipment. If connectivity is unreliable, this should be discussed in advance so an alternative can be arranged."},{question:"Can Nepalis living abroad use this service?",answer:"Yes, subject to what is clinically appropriate and legally permitted for the specific situation and location, which can be discussed when booking. This is particularly relevant for continuity of care or coordinating a transition to a local prescriber abroad."}]},
  {slug:"psychiatric-medications-myths-facts",title:"Psychiatric Medications: Myths and Facts",description:"Psychiatric medications myths and facts: benefits, side effects, antidepressant concerns, safe prescribing and informed decisions with a psychiatrist.",keywords:["Psychiatric medications","Antidepressants myths","Mental health medications"],h1:"Psychiatric Medications: Myths and Facts",overview:"Psychiatric medicines can meaningfully reduce symptoms and help prevent relapse for many people with conditions such as depression, anxiety disorders, bipolar disorder, psychosis and ADHD, but they are not a cure-all, are not appropriate for every presentation, and should never replace a careful diagnostic assessment or an honest, informed conversation between doctor and patient about risks and benefits. Understanding both what a medicine can realistically achieve and what it cannot is central to using it wisely, whether the decision is to start, continue, adjust or eventually stop treatment.",symptoms:"Whether medication is considered at all depends heavily on the underlying diagnosis and its severity, since the same surface symptom, such as poor sleep or low mood, can arise from very different conditions requiring different treatments. This is exactly why self-diagnosing from a symptom checklist and requesting a specific medicine by name, without a proper assessment, risks missing the actual underlying condition. Two people with an identical symptom, such as difficulty sleeping, may need entirely different treatments depending on whether the underlying cause is depression, anxiety, a primary sleep disorder or something else altogether.",causes:"Misinformation about psychiatric medication often stems from fear of dependence, dramatic anecdotes about side effects circulating on social media, cultural beliefs that needing medicine for a mental health condition signals personal weakness, or genuine past experiences of poorly managed treatment. Honest, two-way discussion with a prescriber about these specific fears is far safer and more productive than quietly stopping treatment without medical guidance, which can trigger relapse or, for some medicines, withdrawal effects.",assessment:"Before recommending any medication, a prescriber weighs the diagnosis, physical health and relevant investigations, other medicines being taken, pregnancy or breastfeeding status where relevant, substance use, past response to treatment, and the individual’s own priorities and concerns. This is a genuinely individualised decision; there is no single medicine that is right for everyone with a given diagnosis, and reasonable alternatives usually exist. Cost and access to a particular medicine within Nepal are also practical factors that a thoughtful prescriber will discuss openly rather than assume are unimportant to the patient’s decision.",treatment:"Patients are encouraged to ask what a proposed medicine is actually for, what benefit to realistically expect and over what timeframe, common and serious side effects, available alternatives, what monitoring will be involved, and when the treatment will be formally reviewed. No psychiatric medication is universally appropriate, and medicines should never be shared with another person, started informally, or stopped abruptly without medical advice, since doing so can cause withdrawal effects or relapse.",local:"Patients are encouraged to bring a complete, current medicine list to every appointment in Kathmandu or online follow-up, including anything obtained without a prescription, so interactions and duplications can be checked properly. Any urgent adverse reaction, suspected overdose, or severe confusion after starting or stopping a medicine needs immediate in-person medical care rather than waiting for the next scheduled review.",related:["/contact","/depression","/resources/antidepressants-ssri-snri-guide"],faqs:[{question:"Are psychiatric medications addictive?",answer:"Most antidepressants and antipsychotics are not addictive in the sense of craving or compulsive use, though some can cause withdrawal effects if stopped abruptly, which is different from addiction. A small number of medication classes, such as benzodiazepines, do carry genuine dependence risk and are prescribed more cautiously."},{question:"Will I have to take psychiatric medication forever?",answer:"Not necessarily. Duration of treatment depends on the diagnosis, severity, and individual history, and many people take medication for a defined period before tapering off under medical guidance, while others with recurrent or chronic conditions benefit from longer-term treatment reviewed periodically."},{question:"Do psychiatric medications change your personality?",answer:"Well-chosen psychiatric medication aims to relieve specific symptoms, such as low mood or excessive anxiety, restoring someone closer to their usual self rather than fundamentally changing their personality. Side effects that feel like personality change should always be reported and reviewed with the prescriber."},{question:"Is it true that antidepressants stop working over time?",answer:"For some people, a previously effective antidepressant can feel less effective after a period, and this is worth discussing with a prescriber rather than assuming it is permanent, since dose adjustment, switching or augmentation are all reasonable options to explore."},{question:"Can I just stop taking my medication once I feel better?",answer:"Stopping abruptly, especially without medical guidance, can cause withdrawal effects for some medicines and significantly increases the risk of relapse for many conditions. Any decision to reduce or stop medication should be planned and reviewed with the prescriber."}]},
  {slug:"ocd-common-misconceptions",title:"OCD Myths and Facts",description:"OCD myths and facts: intrusive thoughts, compulsions, ERP therapy, family support and evidence-based OCD treatment in Nepal.",keywords:["OCD myths","Obsessive compulsive disorder","OCD facts"],h1:"OCD: Common Misconceptions",overview:"OCD is often trivialised in everyday conversation as simply liking things neat or organised, but the clinical condition is very different: it involves intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images or urges (obsessions) that cause significant distress, combined with repetitive behaviours or mental rituals (compulsions) performed to reduce that distress or prevent a feared outcome, consuming significant time or causing real impairment. OCD is recognised as one of the more disabling psychiatric conditions when untreated, precisely because the rituals it drives can come to dominate hours of a person’s day.",symptoms:"Common presentations include contamination fears with washing or cleaning rituals, checking behaviours around safety such as locks or appliances, a need for symmetry or exactness, unwanted taboo or violent thoughts that deeply distress the person precisely because they conflict with their own values, counting or repeating rituals, excessive reassurance-seeking, and avoidance of triggering situations. It is important to understand that intrusive thoughts are not intentions, desires, or reflections of a person’s true character; the distress they cause is itself evidence the person does not want to act on them.",causes:"OCD reflects genuine differences in threat-detection and habit-learning circuits in the brain, not poor parenting, insufficient willpower, or a lack of religious or moral discipline. Compulsions briefly reduce the anxiety triggered by an obsession, which powerfully reinforces the ritual and makes it more likely to be repeated the next time the obsession occurs, gradually strengthening the OCD cycle over time even though each individual ritual feels like it is providing relief. There is no evidence that OCD results from a single traumatic event or a specific parenting style, although significant stress can sometimes trigger the onset or a worsening of symptoms in someone already vulnerable.",assessment:"Assessment carefully distinguishes OCD from ordinary worry, perfectionistic personality traits, psychotic disorders, tic disorders, and autism-related routines and repetitive behaviours, since each has a different treatment approach. Because obsessions, particularly taboo or violent intrusive thoughts, often carry significant shame, a non-judgemental, confidential consultation is essential to allow honest disclosure, which is frequently delayed by years due to fear of being misunderstood. Standardised severity scales can help track symptoms once treatment begins, providing an objective measure alongside the person’s own sense of progress.",treatment:"Exposure and response prevention (ERP), a specific and well-evidenced form of CBT, is the first-line psychological treatment for OCD: it involves gradually and deliberately facing feared triggers while resisting the urge to perform the accompanying compulsion, allowing anxiety to naturally decline and teaching the brain that the feared catastrophe does not occur. Medication, typically an SSRI often at higher doses than used for depression, may be considered for some patients after individual assessment, frequently alongside ERP. Family members who have become drawn into participating in rituals, known as family accommodation, are gently supported to reduce this involvement as part of treatment.",local:"ERP can be planned and delivered through a structured course in Kathmandu, with appropriate video-based follow-up supporting people living elsewhere in Nepal, since much of the exposure work can be planned and reviewed collaboratively even between in-person sessions. Group psychoeducation sessions for family members are sometimes offered alongside individual therapy, helping relatives in Kathmandu and beyond understand how best to support recovery without inadvertently reinforcing the rituals.",related:["/ocd","/contact","/erp-therapy-ocd"],faqs:[{question:"Does having intrusive thoughts about harming someone mean I actually want to?",answer:"No. Intrusive thoughts in OCD are, by definition, unwanted and distressing precisely because they conflict with the person’s actual values and intentions; the distress itself is a key diagnostic feature, distinguishing them from genuine intent. This is one of the most common and most reassuring points clarified during assessment."},{question:"Is OCD just about cleanliness and organisation?",answer:"No, contamination and cleaning is only one of several common presentations. OCD can also involve checking, symmetry, taboo intrusive thoughts, counting rituals, and many other patterns entirely unrelated to cleanliness, all unified by the same obsession-compulsion cycle."},{question:"Can OCD be cured completely with treatment?",answer:"Many people who complete a full course of ERP experience a substantial and lasting reduction in symptoms, and some become largely symptom-free, while others retain some vulnerability that is well managed with occasional booster sessions. Individual outcomes vary, which is why a personalised assessment and follow-up plan matter."},{question:"Why does reassurance from family make OCD worse over time?",answer:"Reassurance temporarily reduces anxiety in the same way a compulsion does, which reinforces the obsession-compulsion cycle rather than resolving it. This is why treatment often involves guiding family members to reduce reassurance-giving and other accommodating behaviours, done gradually and supportively."},{question:"How long does ERP therapy usually take?",answer:"This varies by symptom severity and the number of obsessions being addressed, but many structured ERP courses run over a period of weeks to a few months, with progress reviewed regularly and the hierarchy of exposure exercises adjusted as treatment progresses."}]},
  {slug:"bipolar-disorder-vs-depression",title:"Bipolar Disorder vs Depression",description:"Bipolar disorder versus depression: key differences, mania symptoms, diagnosis, treatment and urgent warning signs explained for patients in Nepal.",keywords:["Bipolar vs depression","Mood disorders","Mania symptoms"],h1:"Bipolar Disorder vs Depression",overview:"Bipolar depression can look identical to major depression on the surface, sharing symptoms such as low mood, loss of interest and fatigue, but bipolar disorder also involves distinct periods of mania or hypomania: episodes of unusually elevated, expansive or irritable mood combined with increased energy and activity. Correctly identifying which condition is present matters enormously, because certain antidepressant treatments that help pure depression can potentially trigger a manic episode or worsen the overall course if bipolar disorder is present but unrecognised.",symptoms:"During mania or hypomania, a person may need much less sleep without feeling tired, speak rapidly and jump between topics, experience racing thoughts, form grandiose plans, engage in risky spending or impulsive decisions, and show impaired judgement, sometimes alongside irritability rather than obvious euphoria. During depressive episodes, symptoms resemble major depression closely: persistent low mood, exhaustion, hopelessness, poor concentration, and, in more severe episodes, thoughts of suicide, which always require prompt attention. Mixed features, where symptoms of depression and mania occur close together or even simultaneously, such as agitation alongside low mood, can also occur and add complexity to the clinical picture.",causes:"Bipolar disorder has strong biological and inherited influences, with genetics playing a more prominent role than in many other psychiatric conditions, and specific triggers such as significant stress, substantially disrupted sleep, and substance use can precipitate episodes in people who are already vulnerable. It is a lifelong condition for most who are diagnosed, but one that can be well managed with consistent, individualised treatment. Substance use, particularly stimulants or heavy alcohol use, can both mimic bipolar symptoms and genuinely destabilise mood in someone with the underlying condition, which is why substance use is always reviewed carefully.",assessment:"Diagnosis requires building a careful timeline of mood, sleep, energy and behaviour over months to years, often significantly aided by input from family members who may have observed episodes the patient does not fully recognise as unusual, particularly milder hypomanic periods. Assessment specifically distinguishes bipolar disorder from depression occurring alone, ADHD, substance-induced mood changes, and psychotic disorders, since each has a meaningfully different treatment pathway. Mood charting, where the patient records mood, sleep and energy daily over several weeks, can be a valuable tool both for diagnosis and for monitoring treatment response over time.",treatment:"Mood-stabilising medication is typically central to reducing the frequency and severity of future episodes, alongside protecting a consistent sleep schedule, structured psychoeducation about early warning signs of relapse, psychotherapy, and involving family in relapse-prevention planning where appropriate. Any decision about antidepressant use requires careful individual psychiatric assessment when bipolarity is suspected or confirmed, since antidepressants are generally used cautiously, if at all, without an accompanying mood stabiliser.",local:"Severe mania, an inability to sleep for multiple consecutive days, dangerous or highly impulsive behaviour, or any features of psychosis require urgent in-person psychiatric assessment in Nepal rather than a routine or online appointment, given the safety risks involved in an acute manic episode. Once a patient is stabilised, regular follow-up, whether in person or online, plays a significant role in catching early warning signs of relapse before a full episode develops.",related:["/bipolar-disorder","/depression","/conditions/bipolar-i-disorder"],faqs:[{question:"How can I tell the difference between normal high spirits and hypomania?",answer:"Normal good mood is proportionate to circumstances and does not significantly impair judgement or functioning. Hypomania involves a distinct, sustained change from a person’s usual self, lasting days, with reduced need for sleep, increased activity and often some impaired judgement, that others around the person often notice too."},{question:"Can bipolar disorder be misdiagnosed as depression?",answer:"Yes, this is common, particularly when a person seeks help during a depressive episode and past hypomanic periods were mild, brief, or not recognised as unusual at the time. This is why assessment specifically asks about past periods of elevated mood or energy, sometimes with family input."},{question:"Is it dangerous to take antidepressants if I have bipolar disorder?",answer:"Antidepressants used alone, without a mood stabiliser, can potentially trigger a manic episode or destabilise the overall mood pattern in someone with bipolar disorder, which is why accurate diagnosis before starting treatment matters greatly."},{question:"Does bipolar disorder require lifelong medication?",answer:"Many people with bipolar disorder benefit from long-term mood-stabilising treatment to reduce relapse risk, given how disruptive and sometimes dangerous full episodes can be. This is an individualised decision made with a psychiatrist based on episode history and severity, reviewed periodically."},{question:"Can stress or poor sleep trigger a manic episode?",answer:"Yes, significant sleep disruption and major stress are recognised triggers for both manic and depressive episodes in people with bipolar disorder, which is why protecting sleep and having a plan for high-stress periods is an important part of long-term management."}]},
  {slug:"schizophrenia-early-warning-signs",title:"Early Signs of Schizophrenia",description:"Early signs of schizophrenia and psychosis: hallucinations, delusions, first-episode assessment, family guidance and urgent care in Nepal.",keywords:["Early signs schizophrenia","Psychosis symptoms","First episode psychosis"],h1:"Schizophrenia: Early Warning Signs",overview:"Psychosis refers to a group of experiences involving hearing or seeing things others do not, holding fixed unusual beliefs not shared by one’s community, confused or disorganised thinking, or major, uncharacteristic changes in behaviour, and it can arise from several different underlying conditions. Early assessment matters because timely, appropriate treatment during a first episode is associated with better longer-term functional outcomes, and because the range of possible causes needs to be properly investigated rather than assumed.",symptoms:"Early warning signs can include gradual social withdrawal, a noticeable decline in school or work performance, changes in sleep pattern, increasing suspiciousness or unusual sensitivity to others’ intentions, unusual perceptual experiences, reduced attention to personal hygiene and self-care, disorganised or hard-to-follow speech, and strongly held beliefs that others around the person cannot share or make sense of. Sudden, severe confusion developing rapidly is different from this gradual pattern and constitutes a medical emergency requiring urgent evaluation.",causes:"Psychosis can occur as part of schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, severe depression with psychotic features, substance intoxication or withdrawal, and various medical or neurological conditions, which is precisely why a thorough assessment is needed rather than assuming schizophrenia by default. It is important for families to know clearly that nothing a parent or family member did causes schizophrenia; it is a brain-based condition with genetic and neurodevelopmental contributing factors. A family history of schizophrenia or related psychotic disorders increases likelihood but does not guarantee it will occur, and many people with such a family history never develop the condition themselves.",assessment:"Assessment covers the time course and pattern of symptoms, immediate safety, current medicines and substance use, mood symptoms that might indicate an underlying mood disorder rather than primary schizophrenia, physical health, and detailed observations from family who often notice changes well before the affected person themselves does. A first episode of psychosis typically also warrants medical evaluation, including relevant investigations, to look for treatable physical contributors. Brain imaging or blood tests may be used selectively to rule out other causes, particularly when the presentation is unusual or there are additional neurological symptoms.",treatment:"Treatment typically involves individualised antipsychotic medication chosen and monitored carefully by a psychiatrist, CBT adapted specifically for psychosis, family psychoeducation and intervention, support to reduce any contributing substance use, and structured rehabilitation to support return to education, work or independent living. When approaching someone experiencing psychosis, speaking calmly, avoiding confrontation about the content of their beliefs, and focusing on safety while arranging professional care tends to be more helpful than direct argument or dismissal.",local:"If there is any danger to the person or others, severe self-neglect, significant confusion, aggression, or an inability to remain safe, the nearest emergency department should be used immediately rather than waiting for a scheduled psychiatric appointment, given the acute risks that can accompany untreated psychosis. Once safely stabilised, ongoing psychiatric follow-up, whether in person in Kathmandu or through supported online review, remains important for monitoring symptoms, medication response and overall functioning over the longer term.",related:["/schizophrenia","/contact"],faqs:[{question:"Does hearing voices always mean someone has schizophrenia?",answer:"No. Unusual perceptual experiences like hearing voices can occur in several conditions, including severe depression, bipolar disorder, extreme stress, sleep deprivation and substance use, not only schizophrenia, which is exactly why a full psychiatric assessment is needed to determine the underlying cause."},{question:"Can schizophrenia be treated successfully?",answer:"Yes, with appropriate individualised treatment, many people with schizophrenia achieve meaningful symptom control and are able to work, study, maintain relationships and live independently, particularly when treatment begins early and continues consistently, though the course varies between individuals."},{question:"Did something my family did cause this illness?",answer:"No. Schizophrenia is a brain-based condition with genetic and neurodevelopmental contributing factors; it is not caused by parenting style, family conflict, or anything a family member did or failed to do, and this reassurance is an important part of family psychoeducation."},{question:"How should I talk to a family member who is experiencing psychosis?",answer:"Speaking calmly, avoiding direct confrontation about the content of unusual beliefs, not dismissing their distress, and focusing on safety while arranging professional assessment tends to be more helpful than arguing about whether their experiences are real."},{question:"Is early treatment for psychosis really more effective than delayed treatment?",answer:"Evidence generally supports that timely treatment during a first episode of psychosis is associated with better functional outcomes over time compared with delayed treatment, which is a key reason early warning signs should prompt assessment rather than a wait-and-see approach."}]},
  {slug:"mental-health-screening-tools-explained",title:"Mental Health Screening Tools Explained",description:"Mental health screening tools explained: PHQ-9, GAD-7, depression and anxiety screening, limits of online tests and when to seek help in Nepal.",keywords:["Mental health screening","PHQ-9","GAD-7"],h1:"Mental Health Screening Tools Explained",overview:"Screening questionnaires such as the PHQ-9 for depression and the GAD-7 for anxiety are brief, standardised tools that help identify symptoms worth discussing further with a clinician. They are genuinely useful as a starting point or a way to track change over time, but they are not, by themselves, a diagnosis, and results should never be used alone to start or stop medication or to dismiss a person’s concerns simply because a score falls below a particular threshold.",symptoms:"These questionnaires typically ask about the frequency of symptoms such as low mood, anhedonia (loss of interest or pleasure), excessive worry, sleep disturbance, appetite change, concentration difficulty, and thoughts of self-harm over a recent time period, usually the past two weeks. The resulting score is only one piece of information; a person’s actual circumstances, degree of functional impairment, and full clinical history carry at least as much weight in forming an accurate picture.",causes:"Scores on these tools can be genuinely influenced by physical illness, recent grief or major life stress, language and cultural factors affecting how questions are interpreted, and even the particular day or mood state in which the questionnaire happens to be completed. This variability is exactly why a single score is treated as a prompt for further conversation rather than as a definitive result. Translation and cultural adaptation of these questionnaires into Nepali also matters, since a phrase that captures a symptom precisely in English may not translate with exactly the same meaning or emotional weight.",assessment:"A qualified clinician interprets the screening score alongside a full clinical interview, a specific and direct assessment of safety, and consideration of possible physical or medical contributors to the reported symptoms. Any elevated score on the self-harm item of a questionnaire like the PHQ-9 needs prompt, direct human follow-up rather than being left for a routine future appointment, regardless of the overall total score.",treatment:"Depending on what the fuller clinical picture shows, screening may lead to simple psychoeducation and monitoring, structured psychotherapy, practical lifestyle support, further medical investigation, or psychiatric treatment including medication when appropriate. Screening tools are valuable for tracking progress over the course of treatment, but they never replace emergency care for a person at immediate risk, regardless of what any calculated score suggests. Repeating the same tool at intervals, rather than relying on a single measurement, gives a more reliable picture of whether symptoms are improving, stable or worsening over the course of care.",local:"Using a screening tool can be a reasonable, low-barrier first step for people in Nepal wondering whether their symptoms warrant professional attention, followed by a full consultation in Kathmandu or online from elsewhere in the country when symptoms are persistent, distressing or impairing daily life. Screening can also be a useful way for family members to gently open a conversation with a loved one who may be reluctant to discuss their symptoms directly.",related:["/mental-health-screening","/contact"],faqs:[{question:"Can an online screening tool diagnose depression or anxiety?",answer:"No. These tools identify symptoms that may warrant further evaluation, but a diagnosis requires a full clinical assessment by a qualified clinician who considers the person’s complete history, safety and circumstances, not a questionnaire score in isolation."},{question:"What does a high score on a screening tool actually mean?",answer:"It suggests the presence and severity of certain symptoms during the assessed time period and indicates that a clinical conversation is warranted. It does not, by itself, confirm a specific diagnosis or dictate a specific treatment such as medication."},{question:"I scored high on the self-harm question but do not feel unsafe right now. Should I still get help?",answer:"Yes. Any positive response to a self-harm item deserves prompt follow-up with a clinician or trusted person, even if the immediate risk feels low, since these questions are designed to open a conversation that a total score alone would not capture."},{question:"Can these tools be used to track progress during treatment?",answer:"Yes, screening tools like the PHQ-9 and GAD-7 are commonly used at intervals during treatment to track symptom trends over time, which can help a clinician and patient assess whether the current treatment plan is working as intended."},{question:"Are these screening tools accurate for everyone?",answer:"They are validated in large populations but can be affected by language, cultural context, physical illness or the specific circumstances of the person completing them, which is why a clinician interprets the score in context rather than treating it as an absolute measure."}]},
  {slug:"mobile-overuse-technology-addiction-nepal",title:"Mobile Overuse and Technology Addiction in Nepal",description:"Mobile overuse and technology addiction in Nepal: signs of problematic smartphone and social-media use in students and young adults, causes and treatment.",keywords:["Mobile addiction Nepal","Smartphone overuse","Technology addiction students"],h1:"Mobile Overuse and Technology Addiction in Nepal",overview:"Most people now spend several hours a day on a smartphone, and heavy use on its own is not a disorder or a cause for alarm. Problematic mobile or social media use is instead defined by a genuine loss of control combined with real functional harm, such as missed sleep, neglected responsibilities, and significant distress when separated from the device, rather than by the raw number of hours logged each day. Nepal has seen very rapid growth in smartphone and mobile data access over recent years, which means families and schools are often navigating these questions with comparatively little established local guidance to draw on.",symptoms:"Warning signs include losing track of time while using the phone, compulsively checking it even without a notification prompting the behaviour, irritability or anxiety when it is unavailable or the battery is low, declining school or work performance, consistently delayed bedtime due to phone use, and recurring conflict with family or partners over screen habits. A useful marker is whether the person has tried to cut back and found it genuinely difficult, not simply whether they use the phone often.",causes:"Notifications, infinite scrolling feeds, short-form video and game mechanics are deliberately engineered around variable, unpredictable rewards and novelty, which strongly reinforces repeated checking behaviour in a way that is not accidental. For many users, the phone also offers a ready escape from anxiety, low mood, boredom or loneliness, and constant social comparison through curated posts and feeds can independently worsen self-esteem, adding another layer to the pattern beyond simple habit.",assessment:"Assessment looks well beyond raw screen-time statistics to genuine functional harm across sleep, relationships, and school or work performance, and specifically screens for underlying depression, anxiety and ADHD, since problematic phone use very often accompanies, or is a visible symptom of, one of these conditions rather than existing as a completely separate, standalone problem. Assessment also considers the person’s age and developmental stage, since what counts as problematic use for a young child differs considerably from what is reasonable for an older teenager or adult managing work and study demands online.",treatment:"Useful steps include collaborative, realistic limits set together with the person rather than punitive bans imposed unilaterally, scheduled offline periods, active notification management, and CBT-based strategies that target the specific urges and emotional states driving excessive use. Treating any underlying anxiety, depression or ADHD identified during assessment is usually central to producing lasting change, since addressing the phone use alone while leaving the underlying driver untreated tends to produce only short-term improvement.",local:"Families in Kathmandu and across Nepal can start with one shared, realistic change, such as phone-free meals or a fixed device-free period before sleep, and build from there. A psychiatric assessment is worth arranging when a student’s or young adult’s functioning at school, work or home is clearly and persistently suffering despite these initial efforts. Setting a good example through parents’ own device habits at home is often as influential as any rule placed specifically on a child or teenager.",related:["/conditions/internet-addiction","/adhd","/contact"],faqs:[{question:"How many hours of phone use per day counts as addiction?",answer:"There is no fixed hour threshold that defines addiction. What matters clinically is loss of control over use and genuine harm to sleep, relationships, or responsibilities, not the raw number of hours, since two people with similar usage patterns can have very different levels of impairment."},{question:"Can removing the phone completely solve the problem?",answer:"Sudden, complete removal often causes significant distress and conflict without addressing the underlying reasons for the behaviour, such as anxiety or loneliness the phone was helping to manage. Gradual, collaborative limit-setting alongside understanding the underlying need tends to work better."},{question:"Is social media specifically worse than other phone use?",answer:"Social media’s design around social comparison, curated content and unpredictable rewards through likes and comments can be particularly reinforcing and can specifically affect self-esteem and mood, which is one reason it is often examined separately from general phone use during assessment."},{question:"Can problematic phone use be a symptom of an underlying condition rather than the main problem?",answer:"Yes, this is common. Anxiety, depression and ADHD can all drive excessive phone use as a coping mechanism or through difficulty with attention and impulse control, so assessment looks for these underlying conditions rather than treating the phone use in isolation."},{question:"What is a reasonable first step for a family concerned about a teenager's phone use?",answer:"Starting with one specific, shared and consistently enforced change, such as no phones at the dinner table or charging devices outside the bedroom overnight, tends to be more sustainable than an immediate, comprehensive set of new rules imposed all at once."}]},
  {slug:"gutkha-tobacco-addiction-treatment-nepal",title:"Gutkha and Smokeless Tobacco Addiction Treatment Nepal",description:"Gutkha and smokeless tobacco addiction treatment in Nepal: dependence symptoms, oral health risks, assessment and evidence-based support to quit.",keywords:["Gutkha addiction Nepal","Smokeless tobacco Nepal","Paan masala addiction treatment"],h1:"Gutkha and Smokeless Tobacco Addiction Treatment in Nepal",overview:"Gutkha, paan masala and other smokeless tobacco products are widely available, inexpensive and socially normalised in many parts of Nepal, which means the nicotine dependence they cause is frequently under-recognised as a genuine addiction compared with cigarette smoking or alcohol use, even though the underlying dependence and health risks are serious and real. Public health data from South Asia consistently shows smokeless tobacco use contributing significantly to oral disease burden, underscoring why it deserves the same clinical seriousness as cigarette smoking rather than being treated as a minor habit.",symptoms:"Dependence typically presents as strong, recurring craving between uses of the product, needing progressively larger or more frequent amounts to achieve the same effect, irritability or restlessness when unable to use it, repeated unsuccessful attempts to cut down or stop, and continued use despite visible warning signs such as mouth ulcers, gum disease, tooth staining or loosening, or other oral changes that the person may already have noticed but not acted on.",causes:"Use often begins at a young age through social normalisation, peer influence, easy availability in small shops, and low cost per use, and continues afterward as a way of managing stress, boredom or social situations. Because the product is chewed rather than smoked, it can feel less serious or less medically concerning to users than cigarette smoking, even though nicotine dependence develops through essentially the same neurochemical pathway regardless of how it is delivered into the body.",assessment:"Assessment covers the pattern and duration of use, the presence and severity of craving and withdrawal symptoms, previous quit attempts and what made them difficult, and any coexisting stress, anxiety or depression that may be maintaining the habit. Any white or red patches, persistent mouth ulcers, unexplained lumps, or unusual bleeding in the mouth require prompt dental or ENT review; this article is educational and cannot assess or rule out oral cancer, which is a serious, well-documented risk of prolonged smokeless tobacco use.",treatment:"Behavioural support, structured quit planning with a specific target date, and relapse-prevention strategies for identified triggers form the core of treatment, and nicotine replacement therapy or other pharmacological aids may be considered for some individuals after individual assessment. Combining psychiatric support for the dependence itself with a dedicated dental or medical check for oral health is strongly advisable rather than optional, given the physical risks specific to smokeless tobacco. Identifying and planning around specific triggers, such as particular social settings or times of day when use is strongest, helps make a quit attempt more realistic and sustainable.",local:"Patients in Kathmandu or elsewhere in Nepal can begin with a psychiatric assessment focused on the dependence itself, alongside a referral to a dentist or ENT specialist for examination of any oral symptoms found, since the two aspects of care are best addressed together rather than separately. Family involvement can be particularly valuable in Nepal, where product availability at home or shared use among family members can otherwise undermine an individual’s efforts to quit.",related:["/conditions/nicotine-dependence","/contact"],faqs:[{question:"Is gutkha less addictive than smoking cigarettes because it is not inhaled?",answer:"No. Smokeless tobacco products deliver nicotine through the lining of the mouth and produce genuine physical dependence through the same neurochemical pathway as smoking, even though the route of use feels different and is sometimes wrongly assumed to be less serious."},{question:"What are the main health risks of long-term gutkha or paan masala use?",answer:"Risks include gum disease, tooth damage, oral submucous fibrosis, and oral cancer, among other harms, alongside the general risks of nicotine dependence. Any persistent mouth ulcer, white or red patch, lump, or unusual bleeding should be examined promptly by a dentist or ENT specialist."},{question:"Can nicotine replacement therapy help with gutkha addiction specifically?",answer:"Nicotine replacement therapy and other pharmacological aids may be considered for some individuals as part of a quit plan after an individual assessment, alongside behavioural support, though the right approach depends on the pattern and severity of use."},{question:"Why is it so hard to quit even when someone wants to?",answer:"Nicotine produces genuine physical dependence, meaning stopping causes real craving and withdrawal symptoms such as irritability and restlessness, not simply a habit that can be broken through willpower alone. Structured support significantly improves the chances of a successful, lasting quit attempt."},{question:"Do I need to see a dentist even if my mouth feels fine?",answer:"Yes, a baseline dental or ENT check is advisable for anyone with a history of regular smokeless tobacco use, since some early changes in the mouth are not painful or obviously noticeable to the person themselves and are better identified through professional examination."}]},
  {slug:"sending-psychiatric-medications-abroad-nepal",title:"Sending Psychiatric Medications Abroad: Guidance for Nepali Families",description:"Guidance for Nepali families on continuing psychiatric medication abroad: import rules, documentation, safe practices and arranging local prescribing.",keywords:["Send medication abroad Nepal","Psychiatric medication travel","Nepali students abroad medication"],h1:"Sending Psychiatric Medications Abroad: Guidance for Nepali Families",overview:"Families in Nepal often want to help a relative continue an established psychiatric medication after that person moves abroad to study or work, but controlled and prescription psychiatric medicines are subject to strict, country-specific import, customs and prescribing rules, and a medicine that is entirely routine and legal in Nepal may be restricted, require special permits, or even be illegal to bring into another country without proper documentation.",symptoms:"This article addresses a practical and legal risk rather than a clinical condition: the possibility of medication being confiscated at customs, a trip being delayed or disrupted, or a traveller facing serious legal difficulty because psychiatric medicines, particularly controlled substances such as certain stimulants, benzodiazepines or sedatives, were carried or posted internationally without first checking and complying with the destination country’s specific rules. These consequences can range from a minor delay while customs officials verify documentation to, in more serious cases, the medication being seized entirely and the traveller left without treatment on arrival.",causes:"This kind of difficulty usually arises from an understandable but mistaken assumption that a medicine considered routine in Nepal will be treated the same way everywhere else, or from using informal courier or postal routes to send medication without first verifying whether that specific medicine is classified as a controlled substance at the destination, which varies considerably between countries and can change without notice. Well-meaning relatives already living abroad sometimes offer to help by sending or carrying medication themselves, without realising that the same import restrictions apply to them as to anyone else.",assessment:"Before travel, the destination country’s embassy, consulate, or customs and health authority should be contacted directly to confirm the current, specific rules for that exact medication, since regulations differ significantly between countries and are revised periodically; general guidance cannot substitute for this direct verification, and getting it wrong can carry serious legal or health consequences for the traveller. University or employer international student and staff support offices in the destination country can sometimes provide additional practical guidance specific to that institution’s location.",treatment:"Medication should always be carried in its original, clearly labelled packaging together with the prescription and, ideally, a doctor’s letter describing the diagnosis and treatment in general terms. Controlled psychiatric medicines should never be sent through informal courier or postal channels without first confirming their legal status at the destination, and families should plan well ahead of departure by requesting complete written documentation from the prescribing clinic in good time, since this process can take longer than expected.",local:"For medication needed beyond a short trip, the safest long-term approach is usually to arrange an assessment with a licensed prescriber in the destination country who can prescribe locally, rather than relying indefinitely on medication shipped from Nepal, which carries ongoing legal, logistical and continuity-of-care risks. Dr. Kharel’s clinic in Kathmandu can prepare a written summary and prescription letter to support this transition; contacting the clinic well ahead of travel allows time to arrange this properly.",related:["/online-psychiatrist-nepal","/psychiatrist-for-nepalis-abroad","/contact"],faqs:[{question:"Can I just post psychiatric medication to a family member studying abroad?",answer:"This carries real risk without first confirming the destination country’s specific rules for that exact medication, since many countries strictly regulate the import of controlled psychiatric medicines through informal postal or courier channels, and packages can be confiscated or cause legal difficulty for the recipient."},{question:"What documents should travel with the medication?",answer:"The medication should stay in its original, clearly labelled packaging, accompanied by the current prescription and, ideally, a doctor’s letter describing the diagnosis and treatment in general terms, which can be requested from the prescribing clinic well ahead of the travel date."},{question:"Is it better to find a doctor in the new country instead of relying on medicine from Nepal?",answer:"For anything beyond a short trip, yes, generally. Arranging a local prescriber in the destination country avoids the ongoing legal and logistical risks of relying on medication shipped internationally and also ensures continuity of psychiatric care in the new location."},{question:"How do I find out if my specific medication is restricted in another country?",answer:"The destination country’s embassy, consulate, or customs and health authority should be contacted directly, since rules vary considerably by country and by medication, and can change without notice, so general guidance cannot substitute for this direct verification before travel."},{question:"Can Dr. Kharel's clinic help prepare documentation for a patient moving abroad?",answer:"Yes, the clinic can prepare a written summary and prescription letter to support a transition to care abroad. Contacting the clinic well ahead of the planned travel date allows enough time to prepare this documentation properly."}]},
  {slug:"ketamine-therapy-depression-anxiety-nepal",title:"Ketamine Therapy for Depression and Anxiety: What to Know",description:"Ketamine therapy for depression and anxiety explained: what it is, who it may be considered for, safety considerations and where to access it from Nepal.",keywords:["Ketamine therapy depression","Treatment-resistant depression","Ketamine assisted treatment"],h1:"Ketamine Therapy for Depression and Anxiety: What It Is and What to Know",overview:"Ketamine therapy is a medically supervised treatment sometimes used for severe or treatment-resistant depression, and for certain anxiety presentations, in people for whom standard treatments have not provided adequate relief. It is fundamentally distinct from routine antidepressant treatment in both its mechanism and its delivery, and it is administered only within specialised, closely monitored clinical settings, never as a self-directed, informal, or unsupervised treatment under any circumstances. Interest in ketamine therapy has grown internationally in recent years as research into rapid-acting treatments for severe depression has expanded, though access and regulation vary considerably between countries.",symptoms:"This article is educational rather than diagnostic and is not intended for self-diagnosis; it is most relevant to people who have already tried standard, well-established treatments for depression or anxiety, such as structured psychotherapy and one or more adequately trialled antidepressant medications, without achieving sufficient improvement in their symptoms. It is not intended as a starting point for someone newly experiencing depression or anxiety who has not yet had the opportunity to try established, better-studied treatments first.",causes:"Ketamine is thought to act relatively quickly through the brain’s glutamate signalling system, a mechanism distinct from the serotonin-focused pathway targeted by most conventional antidepressants, which may help explain why some patients notice an effect sooner than with standard medication. Research into exactly how durable this effect is, which patients are most likely to benefit, and how best to sustain any improvement over time is still an active and evolving area of psychiatric research.",assessment:"Ketamine therapy is generally considered only after standard first- and second-line treatments have been adequately tried under specialist supervision and have not produced sufficient benefit. It requires a thorough psychiatric and physical assessment beforehand, including specific screening for conditions where it would not be appropriate, such as certain substance-use disorders or a personal history of psychotic illness, given ketamine’s own psychoactive properties.",treatment:"Because it requires monitored administration by trained staff in a specialised clinical setting, ketamine therapy is not a first-line or standalone treatment and is not suitable for everyone experiencing depression or anxiety. It should always be delivered through an appropriately licensed and monitored service, and used alongside continued, ongoing psychiatric care rather than as a replacement for it, with the treating psychiatrist and the ketamine service ideally coordinating on the overall plan.",local:"Dr. Kharel’s clinic in Kathmandu does not administer ketamine therapy in-house. Patients who may be candidates, based on an inadequate response to standard treatments, can discuss suitability during a psychiatric consultation and be guided or referred toward an appropriately licensed, specialised service that can provide the monitored setting this treatment specifically requires. Patients considering travelling abroad specifically for ketamine therapy are encouraged to discuss this plan with their psychiatrist beforehand so that ongoing care in Nepal can be coordinated around it.",related:["/depression","/online-psychiatrist-nepal","/contact"],faqs:[{question:"Is ketamine therapy the same as recreational or illicit ketamine use?",answer:"No. Medically supervised ketamine therapy uses controlled, monitored dosing in a specialised clinical setting under trained staff, which is fundamentally different from unsupervised recreational use in both safety and clinical purpose. Self-directed use of ketamine for mental health symptoms is not safe and is not what this treatment refers to."},{question:"Who is typically considered a candidate for ketamine therapy?",answer:"It is generally considered for people with severe or treatment-resistant depression, or certain anxiety presentations, who have already adequately tried standard treatments such as psychotherapy and antidepressant medication without sufficient improvement, following a thorough psychiatric and physical assessment."},{question:"How quickly does ketamine therapy work compared to standard antidepressants?",answer:"Some patients notice an effect sooner than with conventional antidepressants, which often take several weeks to show their full benefit, though individual response varies considerably and research into the durability of ketamine’s effects is still evolving."},{question:"Can I get ketamine therapy directly at Dr. Kharel's clinic in Kathmandu?",answer:"No, the clinic does not administer ketamine therapy in-house. Patients who may be candidates can discuss suitability during a consultation and be referred toward an appropriately licensed, specialised service that provides the monitored setting this treatment requires."},{question:"Does ketamine therapy replace ongoing psychiatric care?",answer:"No. It is intended to be used alongside continued psychiatric care rather than in place of it, with coordination between the treating psychiatrist and the specialised ketamine service as part of a comprehensive, ongoing treatment plan."}]},
  {slug:"psychiatric-doctor-kathmandu",title:"Psychiatric Doctor in Kathmandu",description:"Looking for a psychiatric doctor in Kathmandu? Understand what a psychiatrist does, what to expect in a first appointment and how to choose appropriate care.",keywords:["Psychiatric doctor Kathmandu","Psychiatrist Kathmandu","Mental health doctor Kathmandu"],h1:"How to Find a Psychiatric Doctor in Kathmandu",overview:"A psychiatrist is a medical doctor with specialist training in mental-health assessment and treatment. A consultation can help when emotional symptoms, sleep, substance use, unusual experiences or changes in behaviour are persistent or affecting everyday life.",symptoms:"People seek psychiatric care for low mood, panic, repeated worry, OCD symptoms, poor sleep, attention concerns, mood changes, alcohol or drug concerns, hallucinations or distress that feels difficult to manage alone. You do not need to wait until a problem becomes a crisis.",causes:"Mental-health symptoms may be linked to stress, life events, family vulnerability, physical illness, medicines, alcohol or drugs, or a combination. A careful assessment avoids assuming that every symptom has one simple cause.",assessment:"A first appointment usually covers your main concerns, symptoms, previous treatment, medicines, physical health, sleep, substance use, family history and safety. The clinician may recommend medical tests, therapy, follow-up or referral when needed.",treatment:"The right plan depends on the person. It may include clear information, psychotherapy, practical changes, family involvement with consent, medication where appropriate, or a combination. Good care includes discussion of expected benefits, risks and follow-up.",local:"For in-person consultation, the clinic is in Kalanki, Kathmandu. Online consultation may be suitable for selected non-emergency concerns; immediate safety risks, severe confusion or urgent physical symptoms need emergency in-person care.",faqs:[{question:"What is the difference between a psychiatrist and a psychologist or counselor?",answer:"A psychiatrist is a medical doctor who can diagnose mental-health conditions, order or review physical investigations, and prescribe medication when appropriate. Psychologists and counselors provide therapy and assessment but do not prescribe; many patients benefit from both working together."},{question:"Do I need a referral from another doctor to see a psychiatrist in Kathmandu?",answer:"No referral is required. You can contact a psychiatric clinic directly to book a consultation, though a referral or previous medical reports can still be useful background information if you have them."},{question:"What actually happens during a first psychiatric consultation?",answer:"The first visit is mainly a detailed conversation: your current concerns, symptom history, physical health, medicines, sleep, substance use, family history and safety. It is a discussion, not a test you can pass or fail."},{question:"Will a psychiatrist definitely prescribe medication on the first visit?",answer:"Not necessarily. Some presentations warrant starting medication early, while others are better served by further assessment, therapy, or a follow-up visit once a clearer picture has formed. The decision is individualized."},{question:"Is what I discuss with a psychiatrist confidential?",answer:"Yes, confidentiality is a core part of psychiatric care, with narrow, clearly explained exceptions where there is a serious safety risk to you or someone else."},{question:"What should I bring to my first appointment?",answer:"A list of current medicines and doses, any previous reports or diagnoses, and a few written notes about your main symptoms and questions can make the first visit more productive."}],related:["/appointment","/best-psychiatrist-nepal","/online-psychiatrist-nepal"]},
  {slug:"panic-attack-vs-heart-attack",title:"Panic Attack vs Heart Attack",description:"Panic attack versus heart attack: overlapping symptoms, warning signs and why new chest pain needs urgent medical assessment.",keywords:["Panic attack vs heart attack","Chest pain anxiety","Heart attack symptoms Nepal"],h1:"Panic Attack vs Heart Attack: Know the Differences",overview:"Panic attacks and heart attacks can both cause chest discomfort, shortness of breath, sweating, nausea, dizziness and a racing heart. The overlap is real, so symptoms should never be self-diagnosed as anxiety when a heart problem is possible.",symptoms:"A panic attack often includes a sudden rush of fear, trembling, tingling, a sense of unreality or fear of losing control, often peaking within minutes. Heart-attack warning signs can include pressure, squeezing, fullness or pain in the chest; discomfort in an arm, back, neck, jaw or stomach; breathlessness, cold sweat, nausea or light-headedness.",causes:"Panic is a powerful threat-response alarm that can create very real physical sensations. A heart attack occurs when blood flow to heart muscle is severely reduced or blocked. Symptoms alone cannot reliably distinguish the two, and women or people with diabetes may have less typical symptoms.",assessment:"New, severe, persistent or recurrent chest symptoms need urgent medical assessment, especially with fainting, breathlessness, sweating, nausea, pain spreading to the arm, jaw, back or stomach, or known cardiac risk factors. Do not wait for an online psychiatric appointment or try to decide at home.",treatment:"Once urgent physical causes have been assessed, repeated panic attacks can be treated. CBT, including learning not to fear bodily sensations and gradually reducing avoidance, is often helpful. Medication may be considered after an individual psychiatric assessment.",local:"In Nepal, seek urgent care at the nearest emergency department for possible heart-attack symptoms. After medical assessment has ruled out urgent causes, a psychiatric consultation can help with recurring panic, health fear or avoidance.",faqs:[{question:"Can anxiety alone really cause chest pain that feels like a heart attack?",answer:"Yes. Panic attacks can produce genuine chest tightness, a racing heart, breathlessness and sweating through the body's stress-response system, even though no cardiac damage is occurring. That said, this should be confirmed by medical assessment rather than assumed."},{question:"How long do panic attack symptoms usually last compared to a heart attack?",answer:"Panic attacks typically peak within about 10 minutes and ease within 20-30 minutes. Heart attack symptoms often build gradually, may worsen with exertion, and do not reliably resolve on their own, but this pattern is not a safe way to self-diagnose in the moment."},{question:"Should I go to the emergency room if I'm not sure whether it's panic or my heart?",answer:"Yes. Any new, severe, or unexplained chest pain, especially with breathlessness, sweating, pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, or known heart risk factors, should be treated as a possible emergency until medically assessed."},{question:"Are women's heart attack symptoms different, making this harder to tell apart?",answer:"Yes, women and people with diabetes more often have atypical symptoms such as nausea, fatigue, or back or jaw discomfort rather than classic crushing chest pain, which is one more reason not to self-diagnose based on symptoms alone."},{question:"If cardiac tests come back normal, does that mean my chest pain wasn't real?",answer:"No. The pain and fear are genuinely real even when the heart is medically clear. A normal cardiac work-up simply means it's now appropriate to address the anxiety and panic driving the symptoms."},{question:"Can frequent panic attacks affect long-term heart health?",answer:"Chronic, poorly managed anxiety is associated with cardiovascular strain over time, which is one more reason to treat recurring panic attacks rather than simply endure them."}],related:["/anxiety","/appointment"]},
  {slug:"overthinking-treatment-nepal",title:"Overthinking Treatment Nepal",description:"Overthinking treatment in Nepal: understand worry, rumination, anxiety, CBT-based help and when to see a psychiatrist in Kathmandu.",keywords:["Overthinking treatment Nepal","Excessive worrying","Rumination treatment Kathmandu"],h1:"Overthinking: Treatment and Support in Nepal",overview:"People use the word overthinking for worry about the future, replaying the past, repeated doubt or trying to reach complete certainty before acting. It becomes a health concern when it is hard to control and starts affecting sleep, work, study, relationships or mood.",symptoms:"Common signs include mentally replaying conversations, repeatedly researching or asking for reassurance, difficulty making ordinary decisions, muscle tension, irritability, poor concentration and lying awake with thoughts that will not settle. Overthinking can occur with anxiety, depression, OCD, trauma or stress.",causes:"The mind often learns to treat thinking as protection: if every possibility is analysed, perhaps nothing bad will happen. But repeated mental checking can keep the threat system active. Sleep loss, caffeine, uncertainty and major life stress can make the cycle louder.",assessment:"Assessment looks at what the thoughts are about, how much time they take, what you do to neutralise them and whether there are symptoms of anxiety, depression, OCD, trauma, ADHD, substance use or a physical condition. The goal is not to label normal reflection as illness.",treatment:"CBT-based treatment can help identify worry loops, reduce reassurance-seeking and avoidance, practise more balanced attention and build tolerance for uncertainty. Sleep routines, reducing stimulant use and scheduled problem-solving may help. Medication decisions are individual, not automatic.",local:"A consultation in Kathmandu or suitable online follow-up can help create a practical plan. Seek urgent in-person help for self-harm thoughts, inability to stay safe, severe agitation or major loss of functioning.",faqs:[{question:"Is overthinking the same thing as generalized anxiety disorder?",answer:"Not always. Occasional overthinking is common and can occur without meeting the criteria for GAD, which requires excessive, hard-to-control worry across multiple areas of life for six months or more. A clinical assessment can clarify which applies to you."},{question:"Why does trying to just stop thinking about it usually backfire?",answer:"Deliberately suppressing a thought tends to make it more intrusive, not less, a well-documented psychological effect. Structured techniques like scheduled worry time and cognitive restructuring work better than willpower alone."},{question:"Could my overthinking actually be a sign of OCD rather than general anxiety?",answer:"It's possible. If the thoughts are highly repetitive, distressing, and linked to specific rituals or mental compulsions aimed at neutralizing them, OCD is worth assessing separately, since its treatment differs from general anxiety care."},{question:"What's the difference between rumination and useful problem-solving?",answer:"Problem-solving moves toward a decision or action and then stops. Rumination circles the same ground repeatedly without reaching resolution or relief, often replaying the past or rehearsing worst-case futures instead of addressing anything actionable."},{question:"Does poor sleep make overthinking worse, or is it the other way around?",answer:"Both directions are real: sleep loss reduces the brain's capacity to regulate worry, and a mind that won't quiet down disrupts sleep. Treatment usually needs to address both the sleep pattern and the thinking pattern together."},{question:"When does overthinking cross the line into needing professional treatment?",answer:"When it consistently disrupts sleep, decision-making, work, or relationships, or when self-help strategies haven't helped after a genuine attempt, a structured CBT-based assessment is a reasonable next step."}],related:["/anxiety","/mental-health-screening","/appointment"]},
  {slug:"relationship-counselling-nepal",title:"Relationship Counselling Nepal",description:"Relationship counselling in Nepal: when couples or families may benefit, what counselling involves and how mental-health support can help.",keywords:["Relationship counselling Nepal","Couples counselling Kathmandu","Marriage counselling Nepal"],h1:"Relationship Counselling in Nepal",overview:"Relationship counselling offers a structured space to understand recurring conflict, communication breakdown, trust concerns, stress, changing roles or the impact of illness on a partnership. It is not about deciding who is right; it is about helping people understand patterns and make safer, clearer choices.",symptoms:"Couples or families may seek help when arguments repeat without resolution, communication has shut down, intimacy has changed, parenting or financial stress is overwhelming, or anxiety, depression, alcohol use or sleep problems are affecting the relationship. Counselling is not appropriate as a substitute for immediate protection from violence or coercion.",causes:"Relationship strain often reflects more than one cause: work pressure, migration, caregiving, bereavement, family expectations, trauma, mental-health symptoms or substance use can all change how people communicate and cope. Blame usually hides the pattern that needs attention.",assessment:"An initial conversation clarifies each person’s goals, privacy, safety and whether joint or individual work is appropriate. A clinician may recommend individual psychiatric care alongside counselling when mood, anxiety, addiction, psychosis or safety concerns need separate assessment.",treatment:"Useful approaches can build calmer communication, listening, problem-solving, repair after conflict and realistic boundaries. Both people need the freedom to speak safely. When there is fear, threats, violence or control, safety planning and specialist support take priority over joint sessions.",local:"People in Kathmandu can contact the clinic to discuss whether psychiatric assessment, individual therapy, couples counselling or referral is the best next step. Online discussion may be suitable for selected situations where privacy and safety are protected.",faqs:[{question:"Does relationship counselling mean the counselor decides who is right?",answer:"No. The goal is to help both people understand recurring patterns and communicate more clearly, not to assign blame or declare a winner. A skilled clinician stays neutral and focuses on what would actually help the relationship."},{question:"Can counselling still help if only one partner is willing to attend?",answer:"Yes. Individual sessions focused on your own patterns, communication style, and coping can still meaningfully improve a relationship, even without full joint participation, though joint sessions add another dimension when both partners are willing."},{question:"Is relationship counselling appropriate if there is violence or coercive control?",answer:"No, joint counselling is not appropriate or safe when there is violence, threats, or coercive control. Safety planning and individual specialist support take priority, and this should be raised directly and early in any initial conversation."},{question:"How is relationship counselling different from one partner's individual psychiatric care?",answer:"Relationship counselling focuses on the interaction between two people, while individual psychiatric care addresses one person's own mental-health condition. The two are often complementary rather than interchangeable, especially when a diagnosable condition is affecting the relationship."},{question:"What if my partner's depression, anxiety or substance use is the main strain on us?",answer:"That's common, and it's usually addressed on two tracks: individual psychiatric assessment and treatment for the condition itself, alongside couples-focused work on how the relationship has adapted around it."},{question:"Roughly how many sessions does relationship counselling typically take?",answer:"This varies widely with the issues involved, but many couples see meaningful movement within 6-12 structured sessions, with the pace and focus reviewed and adjusted together along the way."}],related:["/counselling-in-nepal","/appointment"]},
  {slug:"health-anxiety-treatment-nepal",title:"Health Anxiety Treatment Nepal",description:"Health anxiety treatment in Nepal: repeated fear of illness, reassurance seeking, medical safety checks and CBT-based support in Kathmandu.",keywords:["Health anxiety treatment Nepal","Illness anxiety Kathmandu","Fear of serious illness"],h1:"Health Anxiety: Treatment and Support in Nepal",overview:"Health anxiety is not pretending or attention-seeking. It is a cycle in which fear of illness becomes so consuming that body checking, online searching, reassurance-seeking or avoiding health information begins to take over daily life. Anxiety itself can create genuine sensations such as a racing heart, headaches or stomach discomfort.",symptoms:"Signs can include repeatedly checking the body, searching symptoms online, repeatedly requesting reassurance, worrying that doctors or tests missed something, avoiding activity for fear of harm or feeling unable to focus on anything else. These patterns can coexist with a real medical condition.",causes:"A frightening illness experience, grief, health information online, stress, OCD vulnerability or a tendency to notice body sensations can all contribute. Checking and reassurance may bring brief relief but can teach the brain to ask for more certainty.",assessment:"A clinician first takes symptoms seriously and considers whether medical assessment is needed. Health anxiety is considered only after looking at the symptom pattern, previous medical information, functional impact and conditions such as OCD, panic, depression and trauma. New or changing symptoms should not be dismissed as anxiety.",treatment:"CBT can help reduce repeated checking and reassurance, test catastrophic predictions and return gradually to important activities. A planned approach with a clinician is safer than alternating between avoiding care and seeking repeated emergency reassurance. Medication may help some people when anxiety is persistent or severe.",local:"If a symptom is severe, new, rapidly worsening or potentially urgent, seek in-person medical care. For ongoing fear that is disrupting life after appropriate medical review, psychiatric care in Kathmandu or suitable online follow-up can help.",faqs:[{question:"How is health anxiety different from just being sensible about my health?",answer:"Sensible health awareness leads to a reasonable check and then lets the worry go. Health anxiety persists despite reassurance, drives repeated checking or searching, and starts to interfere with daily functioning and quality of life."},{question:"If my test results keep coming back normal, why do I still feel convinced something is wrong?",answer:"This is a hallmark of the health-anxiety cycle: reassurance from a normal result brings only brief relief before doubt returns, often stronger. Breaking this cycle usually needs a structured therapeutic approach rather than another test."},{question:"Can health anxiety actually cause real physical symptoms?",answer:"Yes. Anxiety itself produces genuine physical sensations, a racing heart, muscle tension, stomach discomfort, headaches, which can then be misread as evidence of serious illness, reinforcing the fear."},{question:"Should someone with health anxiety just stop going to the doctor?",answer:"No, new or changing physical symptoms should always be medically assessed and never dismissed as 'just anxiety.' The goal of treatment is to reduce excessive, repeated checking and reassurance-seeking, not to avoid legitimate medical care."},{question:"Is health anxiety a form of OCD?",answer:"Health anxiety shares mechanisms with OCD, particularly the checking-and-reassurance cycle, and some people meet criteria for both. A clinical assessment can clarify the most accurate diagnosis and the most effective treatment approach."},{question:"Can health anxiety be treated without medication?",answer:"Often yes, CBT focused on reducing checking and reassurance-seeking and tolerating uncertainty is a first-line, evidence-based treatment. Medication may be added when anxiety is severe, persistent, or significantly impairing."}],related:["/anxiety","/blog/panic-attack-vs-heart-attack","/appointment"]},
  {slug:"do-i-have-hiv-anxiety-testing",title:"Do I Have HIV? Anxiety, Symptoms and Testing",description:"Worried about HIV? Learn why symptoms and anxiety cannot diagnose HIV, when to seek testing and how health anxiety can be supported in Nepal.",keywords:["Do I have HIV anxiety","HIV testing Nepal","HIV symptoms anxiety"],h1:"Do I Have HIV? Testing, Symptoms and Health Anxiety",overview:"Worry about HIV can be frightening, especially after a possible exposure or after reading symptoms online. Neither anxiety nor symptoms can tell you whether you have HIV. The only way to know your HIV status is to take the appropriate test at the right time and discuss the result with a qualified health professional.",symptoms:"Anxiety can cause physical symptoms such as sweating, poor sleep, appetite change, stomach upset, palpitations, headaches and a sense of being unwell. Those symptoms are real, but they are not specific to HIV. HIV can also have no symptoms for a long time, so symptom-checking cannot confirm or exclude infection.",causes:"A possible exposure, stigma, uncertainty, internet searches and repeated reassurance can create a powerful fear cycle. The fear may persist even after a negative result if the person does not understand the test’s window period or begins to interpret every body sensation as evidence of infection.",assessment:"A sexual-health or medical clinician can discuss the type and timing of exposure, testing options, the window period and whether repeat testing is needed. If there was a possible recent exposure, seek prompt medical advice rather than relying on online information. A psychiatrist cannot diagnose HIV from symptoms or provide a substitute for HIV testing.",treatment:"If testing and medical advice show that HIV is not present but the fear continues to dominate daily life, health-anxiety or OCD-focused treatment can help reduce checking, searching and reassurance cycles. This support is not a reason to skip medical testing. If HIV is diagnosed, prompt specialist treatment and ongoing care are important.",local:"Contact an appropriate medical or sexual-health service in Nepal for confidential HIV testing and advice. A mental-health consultation can support severe anxiety alongside—not instead of—medical testing. If you feel unable to stay safe because of fear or shame, seek urgent in-person help.",faqs:[{question:"How soon after a possible exposure should I get tested for HIV?",answer:"Testing timing depends on the type of test used and needs individual medical guidance, this is exactly the kind of question to raise promptly with a medical or sexual-health clinician rather than trying to work out from general information online."},{question:"Can anxiety or stress cause a false HIV test result?",answer:"No, HIV tests detect the virus or the body's immune response to it, not anxiety. Stress does not cause a false positive or false negative; a clinician can explain how a specific test works and its accuracy at a given time point."},{question:"I already tested negative, but I still can't stop worrying, what does that mean?",answer:"This pattern, worry persisting despite a reassuring result, is common and often reflects health anxiety or OCD-like reassurance-seeking rather than an unresolved medical question. It's genuinely treatable, separately from the testing itself."},{question:"Can a psychiatrist tell me whether I have HIV based on my symptoms?",answer:"No. Only an appropriate HIV test, discussed with a medical or sexual-health professional, can answer that question. A psychiatrist's role is supporting the anxiety around this, not replacing testing."},{question:"What is the HIV testing window period and why does it matter?",answer:"It's the time after a possible exposure during which a test may not yet reliably detect infection. A medical or sexual-health clinician can explain the window period for the specific test being used and advise on repeat testing if needed."},{question:"Is it normal to feel intense anxiety while waiting for test results?",answer:"Yes, this is a very common and understandable experience. If the anxiety feels overwhelming or persists well beyond getting a clear result, that ongoing distress itself is a legitimate, treatable concern worth raising with a mental-health professional."}],related:["/blog/health-anxiety-treatment-nepal","/appointment"]},
];

export function getSupportingArticle(slug: string) { return supportingArticles.find((article) => article.slug === slug); }
